WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.290
Hey people welcome to the podcast

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.920
Joshua Fields Millburn and Ryan Nicodemus,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.680
AKA The Minimalists, are in the house dropping truths

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.540
on how to live better with less.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.420
I love these guys.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.860
I've known them for years.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.530
So much respect for their mission, their work,

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:20.250
empowering millions of people all over the world.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.130
This conversation is stellar,

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.150
potentially even life altering.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:29.150
So with that being said, hit the subscribe button and enjoy.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:36.870
What's up, guys?

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:37.830
What's up?

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.520
This is a long time in the making.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.009
Yeah

00:00:41.009 --> 00:00:41.842
It's good to have you here.

00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:43.209
Great to see both of you.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:44.248
Good to be had.

00:00:44.248 --> 00:00:45.650
Joshua's hair is looking perfect as always.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:46.483
Always.

00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:49.020
You know, Joshua gets a lot of attention for his hair

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.480
and this whole Christopher Walken thing,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.047
but not enough attention is paid to your hair, Ryan.

00:00:54.047 --> 00:00:55.746
You have excellent hair

00:00:55.746 --> 00:00:56.660
I'm sort of between you guys
My hair looks horrible today.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:57.493
I cut my hair.

00:00:57.493 --> 00:00:58.770
I had long hair like yours.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.670
But you're looking fabulous today as well.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.028
Thanks, man.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.190
You know, I felt like I was having a bad hair day today

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.030
so that really uplifts my spirits.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.300
So thank you very much
It's all good, man.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.223
It's all good.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.170
People who've been on this podcast journey for a while

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.740
will likely remember a podcast that I did

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.240
with Joshua way back in the day.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.210
But Ryan, this is your first time here.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.580
I was boycotting you at the time.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.070
Yeah, I don't know what was going on between us,

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.870
but we worked it out.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.620
You guys have been gracious enough to host me

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.360
on your show several times.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.700
And I'm delighted to have you here

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.430
on the eve of your new documentary coming out.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:32.400
That's exciting, man.
Thanks so much

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.233
Thanks for having us.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:34.290
Yeah, it's cool.
I'm excited to be

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.550
here too, man.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.980
Your story is well told, but I think it would be good

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.240
to at least contextualize everything

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.962
we're gonna talk about

00:01:42.962 --> 00:01:44.130
by sharing your personal stories.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.200
And also in particular Ryan,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.760
since yours, you haven't had an opportunity

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:49.720
to share yours here.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.480
So how do we launch into that?

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.440
What's the best place to start?

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:54.710
Should we start at the beginning?

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:58.380
Yeah, 1981. (all chuckle)

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.810
Fifth grade, best friends.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.654
I think we should start with Josh.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:02.760
Two pudgy kids sitting in the corner.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:03.593
Yeah, exactly.

00:02:03.593 --> 00:02:05.155
Plotting world domination.

00:02:05.155 --> 00:02:06.890
That's not far from the truth

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.440
except the world domination part.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.390
It was more like

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:11.740
the suburbs
You were working your way

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:12.573
towards it

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.560
of Dayton domination.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.460
Working our way towards it.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.630
How many kids from Dayton have documentaries on Netflix?

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.330
I mean, the Wright brothers.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.650
Yeah.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.340
The Wright brothers, okay, all right

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.350
So there's them.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.320
Who's the other?

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.950
Martin sheen, he is from.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:27.783
I don't think there's a doc though.

00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:28.780
No.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.020
He might be in a documentary.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.150
Here's where we start, I think.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.750
I'll tee it up.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.250
So Ryan and I grew up really poor in Dayton, Ohio.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:38.830
We've known each other, as you said,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.250
since we were fat little fifth graders

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.820
and literally I was the fattest kid in school.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.000
Ryan was the second fattest kid

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:45.860
It's so hard to believe,

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.190
but when you watch the documentary,

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:48.840
you're like, wow, he was telling the truth.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.840
Yeah, I mean, it was impressive or depressive.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.360
Anyway, we knew each other.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.830
We grew up really poor, dysfunctional homes,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.380
before that term dysfunctional was in vogue

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.830
and we were pretty discontented growing up.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:10.460
And of course we thought, well, how do we become happy?

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.670
And of course that was make money.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.160
And so we climbed the corporate ladder

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.080
throughout our twenties

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.950
and had ostensibly successful careers.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:23.310
But of course we weren't successful.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.760
We were overwhelmed and stressed

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.040
and sort of discontented by the lives we created.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.810
And you know, what's fascinating now,

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.460
Ryan, is the the life we were living growing up,

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.063
it was so chaotic.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:42.390
We traded that chaos just for a different type of chaos.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.420
Controlled chaos it's still chaos.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.700
In fact, it's an oxymoron.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.300
And so yeah, we were working in corporate America

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.380
making really good money, spending even better money.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.840
And so had massive amounts of debt

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.060
and knew we needed to make a change.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:03:59.893
Well, yeah.

00:03:59.893 --> 00:04:00.910
I mean, you both grew up

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.140
in alcoholic households, essentially, right?

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:03.973
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:04:03.973 --> 00:04:05.260
Food stamps, poverty.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:09.640
So it's not surprising that you would seek out security

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:13.590
and an upward kind of financially secure trajectory.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.040
I mean, that's what most people would do

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.550
trying to emerge out of a situation like that.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.800
Yeah, I just, I remember working

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.733
for my dad just out of high school.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.070
He just had like small painting and wallpaper company

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:31.630
and he worked his butt off and he made nothing

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.160
and now I was there working my butt off making nothing.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.410
And I'm like, I know there's a way I can work

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.700
just as hard but make more money.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:44.460
And that's when I went into Telecom with Josh.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.690
And yeah, like he said,

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.050
I started making really awesome money.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.020
And I thought, oh, I figured it out.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.050
I finally figured it out.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.200
I remember, 'cause even during high school

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.680
I worked for my dad painting and hanging wallpaper.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.960
And I remember early on when I was working for him

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.780
we were in this really nice house.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:04.440
It wasn't like a mansion or anything,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.470
but it was like something nicer

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.140
than my mom or dad had ever owned.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.107
And I looked at my dad, I'm like,

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:09.477
"This is a really nice place, man.

00:05:09.477 --> 00:05:12.240
"Like how much do I need to make in order to live here?"

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.327
And he's like, "If you can make $50,000 a year

00:05:15.327 --> 00:05:17.820
"you could probably afford a place like this."

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.230
So for me, that was like, yeah,

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:23.700
that was the number for happiness, $50 grand a year.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.760
And when I went to the Telecom company,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.060
I think that very first year

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.660
I made like $52,000 or something.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.250
And I was really proud of myself

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:33.820
and happy that I was making more money.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.150
I went out and bought a new truck

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.250
I guess it wasn't technically mine,

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.490
'cause you know, I got a loan,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.580
got a car payment basically, but I wasn't happy.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.680
And I realized why I wasn't happy.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.570
It was because I didn't adjust for inflation.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.950
The $50,000 number had grown a little bit.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.360
So I'm like, oh, maybe it's $60,000,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.000
maybe it's $80,000, maybe it's six figures

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.900
or maybe just owning a bunch of stuff.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:01.090
So yeah, that's what I pursued.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.590
But I mean, my story really with minimalism,

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.000
it kind of starts with Josh

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.470
because when we were in the corporate world,

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.360
we were miserable.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.979
We had accumulated so many different burdens.

00:06:12.979 --> 00:06:16.660
Whether it's debt or whether it's an overabundance

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.263
of clutter,

00:06:19.441 --> 00:06:22.010
whether it was chasing a job title.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:27.010
I mean it was very, very depressing.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.190
But I noticed Josh who started being

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.550
a little bit less depressed

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.047
and that's when I went to him

00:06:35.047 --> 00:06:35.927
and I asked him like,

00:06:35.927 --> 00:06:38.037
"Hey man, what are you doing that's making you so happy?

00:06:38.037 --> 00:06:39.790
"Why the hell are you so happy?"

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.700
And that's when he introduced this thing

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.710
called minimalism into my life.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:46.470
Well, walk me up to the point of this epiphany Joshua.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.020
'Cause I think it's important to understand

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.490
the inflection point that introduced you

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:52.610
to this new way of living.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:53.743
Sure, yeah.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.770
My mom died and my marriage ended both in the same month.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.050
And so it was like sort of this double car crash.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.770
It's like you get hit by one thing

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.180
and then it swerves you into this other thing.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.290
And at the same time, my corporate career,

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:10.250
I was really discontented by it.

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:13.100
I was managing 150 retail stores,

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.640
which I know is ironic with the whole minimalism thing now,

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:19.810
but maybe it took that in a way for me to recalibrate.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:21.390
But these two big events happened to me

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:24.220
and I started questioning everything.

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:25.900
And literally every thing,

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:27.483
the things in my life, especially

00:07:27.483 --> 00:07:30.340
because I had spent the last dozen years,

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:31.900
I was 30 at the time,

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:36.900
and as I began questioning those things in my life

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.660
I realized that I had worked so hard

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.700
to buy a bunch of things to make me happy

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:46.680
and those things aren't doing their job.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.860
And so they had become my priority though.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.680
And so of course my priorities were totally

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.520
out of whack, achievement, success.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:56.680
Now it's not that Ryan and I are

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.360
against material possessions,

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:02.100
and we don't think it's morally wrong to own things.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.190
We own stuff.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:04.910
I got here in a car today.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:06.473
I have a bed and a couch.

00:08:06.473 --> 00:08:07.306
Wearing a nice jacket.

00:08:07.306 --> 00:08:08.497
Yeah, thank you.

00:08:08.497 --> 00:08:11.146
A lot of money spent on hair products.

00:08:11.146 --> 00:08:13.680
(all laughing)

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.290
But the thing is

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.800
I had such an attachment to the things

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:20.190
but also the perception of other people

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.239
and a lot of that had to do with ego.

00:08:22.239 --> 00:08:27.239
And so ultimately what I figured out at the time

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:30.530
was, oh, my priorities are really out of whack.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:33.110
And thankfully I stumbled across minimalism,

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:34.600
thanks to the internet at the time.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.340
Someone tweeted Colin Wright, a friend of ours,

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.960
who was in our first minimalism documentary.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.900
And he lived with 52 items.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:47.280
And I didn't aspire to that,

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.680
but it made me realize there was a relatively normal person

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.790
doing very abnormal things.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:57.360
And I didn't want to live his life

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.500
but that exposed me to a bunch of other minimalists,

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:03.680
people like Leo Babauta, Courtney Carver, and Joshua Becker,

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.020
more normal people who had like families and houses.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.130
Leo's got like five kids or something like that.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:10.385
Six

00:09:10.385 --> 00:09:11.378
Six.

00:09:11.378 --> 00:09:12.403
Yeah.

00:09:12.403 --> 00:09:13.401
He didn't even have the condoms,

00:09:13.401 --> 00:09:14.403
he was so minimalist

00:09:14.403 --> 00:09:16.020
What's interesting about this

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:18.713
to me is you could have,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.830
given a different set of circumstances,

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:24.900
ended up at a Buddhist monastery or joining the Peace Corps.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:26.850
Clearly you were having an existential crisis

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.430
about how you were living.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:31.470
You had premised your entire life on this idea

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:35.530
that this very traditional upward corporate trajectory

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:38.280
would deliver on its promise of making you happy.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.580
You achieve those things.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.900
You're lacking that sense

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:45.240
of connection that you thought it would deliver

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.550
and you go searching, and you stumble upon minimalism.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:51.420
But what if it had been some other thing?

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:54.290
There are other paths to sort of finding

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:56.230
a little bit more fulfillment and purpose,

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:59.160
but you, for whatever reason,

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.900
minimalism was the thing that you connected with.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:05.300
Yeah and by the way, I think that I connected with it

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:07.000
because there wasn't a particular dogma

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.300
among the people that I saw.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.220
There wasn't a 12 step thing.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.586
And I look at a lot of the stuff now on

00:10:14.586 --> 00:10:16.250
Don't you dare talk bad on the 12 step thing.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:17.083
No, no, no, no.

00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:18.149
Go ahead.

00:10:18.149 --> 00:10:18.982
No, I know I'm just kidding.
I'm not talking

00:10:18.982 --> 00:10:19.815
about 12 steps

00:10:19.815 --> 00:10:21.743
But like the five steps to declutter your closet.

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:27.570
And so it wasn't, the how to side of things

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.500
wasn't that appealing to me,

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.420
it was the why to side of things

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:36.230
that really made me scrutinize what I was doing.

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:38.050
Even now you won't see me and Ryan

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:41.360
talk about the 17 ways to declutter your kitchen.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.992
We're not gonna do a video about that.

00:10:42.992 --> 00:10:44.330
If someone else wants to do that, that's fine.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:46.240
I don't think that actually addresses the problem.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.800
I think those types of solutions,

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.100
they often cause new problems

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:51.703
because we begin to focus on,

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:57.370
we turn those things into the main focus

00:10:57.370 --> 00:10:58.370
and it removes our focus

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:01.680
from whatever the underlying problem is.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:05.830
And so I was fascinated by what these people were doing

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:07.360
and how they were doing it,

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.720
but I was much more focused on the why.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.249
That's why the new film, Less Is Now,

00:11:12.249 --> 00:11:14.660
it really starts with that question.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:17.060
How might your life be better with less?

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.680
Now that's a how question, but it's a disguised question.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:20.880
It's actually a why question.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.870
You see that in the anecdotes

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:26.980
and the stories of the kind of everyday people

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:29.350
that have undergone this process

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:31.620
and how it's emotionally changed their lives

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:34.580
or their perspective on how they live day to day.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:35.413
But it is true.

00:11:35.413 --> 00:11:37.070
And we've talked about this before

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:38.850
that on a surface level

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:40.440
it's about getting rid of your stuff

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.120
but it's really not about that at all.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:44.770
And I think a lot of the focus

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:47.330
or the kind of news narrative around it

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:49.090
is around decluttering,

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.760
but the process of decluttering

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:53.600
is really just a way to clear space

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:57.120
so you can marshal your awareness

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.060
onto the things that are important

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:01.071
that you want to focus on.

00:12:01.071 --> 00:12:02.400
Is that fair or accurate?

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:04.410
Yeah, no, I think that is totally fair.

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:05.243
It's interesting though.

00:12:05.243 --> 00:12:07.840
It makes me think how the media

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.392
wants to sell a solution to a symptom

00:12:11.392 --> 00:12:13.400
rather than address the problem.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.870
I mean, that doesn't really speak to what you're asking.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:18.470
Well, the problem is so massive

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:21.893
and we're all living amidst this grand delusion.

00:12:23.728 --> 00:12:27.360
Our entire society is founded upon the idea

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:29.670
that you played out in your own life

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:30.990
and had to figure out for yourselves,

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:32.260
wasn't delivering on the promise.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:35.300
This idea that we should be seeking security,

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:39.290
and comfort, and salary increases, and the new car,

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:41.650
and all the messages that bombard us

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.810
everywhere we turn our head reinforces that.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:46.950
And yet what it doesn't do,

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:49.730
and the movie does a beautiful job of illustrating this,

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:52.400
is remind us about what's most important

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.350
which is community and the connection

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:55.460
to the people that we love

00:12:55.460 --> 00:13:00.460
and pursuing your life with some level of intentionality.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:02.160
Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.400
Well, I mean, Annie Leonard talks

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:08.740
about this in the documentary about deficit advertising.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:12.410
So advertisers, big corporations, whatever,

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.460
they go out of their way to make you feel

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:17.190
like you're missing something in your life.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:20.340
So that's why we chase the bigger paycheck.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:21.860
So we can go out and buy those things

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:24.180
that subconsciously we're like, oh man,

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.350
if I just had like a little bit of a nicer car

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:27.540
I'd have a better ride to work,

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.880
and I'd enjoy my commute a little bit more.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.720
And if I just a little bit bigger of a house,

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:34.790
I could have that Pilates studio.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:35.827
And I mean, yeah.

00:13:35.827 --> 00:13:37.767
You just notch it up, and then you get there.

00:13:37.767 --> 00:13:40.800
And then you're like, well, I don't feel that way yet,

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:42.050
but it's because there's a guy

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:44.040
who's living a little bit higher

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:44.873
up on the Hill than I am.

00:13:44.873 --> 00:13:45.870
Yeah, by the way

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:48.463
I don't want to moralize any of this either.

00:13:48.463 --> 00:13:50.990
I don't want to say it's better or worse

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:53.183
to own fewer possessions.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:56.580
I'm not saying that it's good

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:59.890
that Ryan and I own less than we used to own.

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:01.633
I'm not saying it's bad either.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:07.240
It is probably more appropriate for us,

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.020
given the constraints.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.860
But to the point you were asking about, Rich,

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:13.350
our material possessions are sort of

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.320
this physical manifestation of what's going on inside us.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.850
That's that existential crisis you talked about.

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:22.400
And so the external clutter is a way

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:25.020
that we sort of visualize the mental clutter,

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:27.710
or the psychological clutter, or the emotional clutter

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:31.010
the spiritual clutter, this internal clutter.

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:33.410
And we found that like with minimalism,

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:35.750
yeah, we could have been Buddhism or Christianity

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:38.890
or anything else that we would have stumbled into

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:41.550
but stumbling into minimalism allowed us

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:44.110
to deal with the thing that had become

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:45.570
this priority in our lives.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:48.250
Stuff had become this priority.

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:50.240
And so it started with the stuff

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:51.970
but that's sort of the initial bite at the apple.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:56.780
And then it goes to simplifying all other areas of life.

00:14:56.780 --> 00:15:00.160
But there's this moment that you experienced

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.960
and that you see in the people profiled in the film

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:05.000
where the lights kind of go on,

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:06.600
as they begin this process,

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:10.190
there's like an and enervating that occurs in their lives.

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:13.240
Like they become very emotionally involved

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:15.350
in the process of decluttering their lives

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:17.330
and it becomes exciting,

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:20.770
and there's a momentum that kind of occurs.

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:23.610
Yeah, you're talking about the everyday minimalists

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.580
that we have in our film.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:27.010
Yeah, no, it is encouraging.

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:28.097
There's one gentleman in there

00:15:28.097 --> 00:15:31.160
who talks about how once he started simplifying

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:34.060
and got to where he felt like he wasn't

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:35.100
living in a bunch of clutter,

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.720
he kind of realized, oh, I've had everything

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:40.060
I ever needed this whole time.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:41.030
He gets emotional.

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:44.010
And he's an older guy, he's not like a millennial.

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.450
Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:49.000
No, I love having those kind of everyday minimalists

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:50.760
in there to kind of talk about their own

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:51.750
personal experiences,

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:53.120
especially because Josh and I,

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:54.970
we can tell her stories all day long,

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:57.810
but you need other people to resonate with

00:15:57.810 --> 00:15:59.993
than just two dudes with really great hair.

00:16:02.328 --> 00:16:04.220
It is really great though.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:06.870
We didn't want it to be the Josh and Ryan story.

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:08.480
Like the first film, Minimalism,

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.380
it was sort of exposing people

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:13.100
to different ideas of intentional living

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:15.120
and different areas of minimalism.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.250
There were minimalist architects

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:18.710
and there are Buddhists, et cetera.

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:20.620
In this film, we didn't want it to just be,

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.580
oh, Josh and Ryan tell their story

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:26.060
but it sort of circled around that initial story of us.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:27.370
But we brought in some experts

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:29.800
but then we also had 30 of those everyday minimalist.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:32.260
So we wanted all these different perspectives.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.960
You probably noticed the diversity in the film

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:37.650
but it's not just like an ethnic diversity.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:39.678
It's like we have a 17 year old

00:16:39.678 --> 00:16:41.087
and a 70 year old in the film.

00:16:41.087 --> 00:16:42.630
And all we did was we put a call out

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:44.670
and said, hey, does anyone have a story

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:47.840
about how they've been affected by our first film,

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:51.090
and they've simplified their lives?

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.137
And how did that work out for you?

00:16:52.137 --> 00:16:55.523
And we just started bringing those people into the film.

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:59.870
So taking it back, you have this experience

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:01.690
in the wake of your marriage dissolving

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:03.650
and your mother's passing,

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:06.450
where you decide that you're gonna basically

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:08.640
change your lifestyle top to bottom.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:10.610
You get rid of all this stuff,

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:13.320
you have that light's going on experience.

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:15.360
You seem lighter in your shoes.

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:18.060
I assume you're still at the job at that point

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:18.970
but you were just showing up

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:20.970
with a little bit of a bigger smile.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:22.660
Yeah, I was also doing a better job

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:23.840
setting expectations.

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:26.060
So we were on call like doctors,

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:27.500
but I wasn't saving anyone.

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:29.170
I couldn't even save myself.

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:33.330
And yet I had to answer the phone at 10:00 PM

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:36.090
if my boss' boss wanted to know something

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:38.490
about the sales transactions for any of the stores that day.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.130
So it was a regular occurrence.

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:42.100
The last thing I did when my head hit the pillow

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.100
was check my Blackberry.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:44.360
The first thing when I got up in the morning

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:45.950
was check my Blackberry.

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:48.350
And throughout the day, all throughout the day,

00:17:48.350 --> 00:17:52.240
I was checking and I started setting expectations.

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:54.750
And that was sacrilegious where we worked.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:55.657
I called my boss, said,

00:17:55.657 --> 00:17:58.120
"I'm not gonna take phone calls in the evening anymore."

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.750
And I could audibly hear his jaw drop

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.383
on the phone because, what do you mean?

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:08.840
You realized that he didn't realize that was an option,

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:10.120
even for himself.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:11.160
I got a call from our boss.

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:11.993
We had the same boss.

00:18:11.993 --> 00:18:15.407
And he was like, "Is Josh, like, is he depressed?

00:18:15.407 --> 00:18:17.600
"Like maybe planning on killing himself or something?"

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:19.650
'Cause I'm giving away my stuff.

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:20.483
Giving away his stuff.

00:18:20.483 --> 00:18:22.530
He's saying, hey, I don't care what you think.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:24.737
I'm not taking phone calls at six.

00:18:24.737 --> 00:18:26.050
But isn't that kind of sad

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:27.350
that that's the reaction though.

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:28.693
Yeah.

00:18:28.693 --> 00:18:30.180
As opposed to, wow, is he doing something

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:32.339
that changes his life

00:18:32.339 --> 00:18:35.883
so that he experiences more peace or more contentment?

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:41.330
Like I said, I don't think this is an indictment on stuff

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.520
or even on corporate America.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:47.410
It's an indictment on chasing, on craving, on attachment.

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:51.453
Well, also on your relationship to externalities.

00:18:52.549 --> 00:18:53.466
Yeah.

00:18:53.466 --> 00:18:54.299
Yeah.

00:18:54.299 --> 00:18:57.810
So, Ryan, at some point you began

00:18:57.810 --> 00:18:59.070
to cotton on to this,

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.020
like what's going on with Joshua, right?

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.610
Yeah, I mean, it was after him setting boundaries.

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.850
It was after that phone call from our boss

00:19:06.850 --> 00:19:08.660
because I did not see a depressed Josh.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:11.210
I saw someone who was taking control

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.183
of something that he had no control over.

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.910
And it wasn't until,

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.210
so he moved into a new place

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:21.120
after he split up with his wife.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:24.030
And it was like a two bedroom, one bath or maybe two baths,

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:26.477
but it was only half full.

00:19:26.477 --> 00:19:28.230
But the one thing that was missing

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:30.280
that really stood out to me was the TV.

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:31.800
And every time I'd go over, I'd be like,

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:33.380
dude when are you getting a TV?

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:34.600
What kind of TV are you gonna get?

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:36.980
Because that's how we compared success

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:38.400
in the corporate world.

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:39.660
What kind of TV you got at your house?

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:40.890
How many TVs do you have?

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:42.213
How big is that TV?

00:19:43.888 --> 00:19:46.128
I wonder what Freud would say about this.

00:19:46.128 --> 00:19:47.272
So at a certain point, he's like,

00:19:47.272 --> 00:19:48.210
"I don't think I'm gonna get a TV."

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.610
I'm like, interesting.

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:54.610
And yeah, eventually I wanted whatever he was doing.

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:59.240
And when he said minimalism, I don't know,

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:00.950
you asked that in the beginning

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:03.830
about would it have been a different path,

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:04.970
could it have been a different path

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.270
if we didn't find minimalism.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:07.300
Well, if he shaved his head

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:10.080
and disappeared to a monastery for a while and came back,

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:11.550
maybe you wouldn't have been so.

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:12.938
Yeah, probably not.

00:20:12.938 --> 00:20:13.923
Probably not.

00:20:14.938 --> 00:20:16.640
But he introduced the term minimalism to me

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:19.010
and to be honest, if it was someone else

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:21.810
I don't know how seriously I would have taken it,

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:24.760
but him and I have very, it's weird,

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:27.680
we have like exact opposite Myers-Briggs personalities

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:30.040
but we have very similar personalities in other ways.

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:32.110
So I was like, if this works for him

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:33.180
I'll at least give it a shot

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.610
and look at it and see if it'll work for me.

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:38.360
So the first video he sent me was Colin Wright

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:39.990
with the 50 things that he owned.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:42.110
And I'm like, oh okay, this is a little weird.

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:43.720
But then I got into other minimalists,

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:46.830
Courtney, Carver, Leo Babauta, Joshua Becker.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.790
And you know, what I saw is common sense.

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:52.790
I saw a lot of people talking

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:56.000
about all these common sense things that I knew internally

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.310
but for some reason I never listened to.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:00.714
And minimalism for me,

00:21:00.714 --> 00:21:03.775
it was an opportunity for me to start over.

00:21:03.775 --> 00:21:05.820
It was an opportunity for me to,

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.220
well, do what Josh did and gain control

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:09.110
of what I had lost control over.

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:10.470
So I got really excited.

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:12.930
I'm like, dude, I want to be a minimalist.

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:14.210
This is awesome.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:16.683
I get it, what should I do?

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:21.290
So you do this packing party thing, basically.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:22.230
Yeah, exactly.

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:24.457
So in the excitement, he was like,

00:21:24.457 --> 00:21:25.623
"Well, what if we pack up all your stuff

00:21:25.623 --> 00:21:28.101
"and then unpack it as you need it."

00:21:28.101 --> 00:21:28.934
And I'm like, "That's a great idea"

00:21:28.934 --> 00:21:31.850
Especially 'cause I'm a very extreme person

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:32.710
when it comes to things.

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:35.280
Like Josh had pared down over several months.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:38.470
He made some very slow changes over months time.

00:21:38.470 --> 00:21:41.210
But for me, like I needed faster results.

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:42.930
So the packing party was perfect for me.

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:46.300
I think probably most of your listeners right now

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:47.430
I don't know if the packing party

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.170
would be the best option for them,

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.050
especially if they've got families and stuff.

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:53.556
Although we have totally seen families do.

00:21:53.556 --> 00:21:54.389
Well, yeah, I'll say this.

00:21:54.389 --> 00:21:56.092
Everyone's at home thinking

00:21:56.092 --> 00:21:57.735
of interesting things to do.

00:21:57.735 --> 00:21:59.130
There's a lot of people rearranging the furniture

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:00.340
in their houses right now,

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:02.089
losing their minds.
That's a really good idea.

00:22:02.089 --> 00:22:02.922
This is like the perfect time

00:22:02.922 --> 00:22:04.200
for them to confront all this stuff.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:06.090
The one time you're really confronted with everything,

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.720
like truly confronted, is when you move to a new house.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:10.310
And so that was sort of the impetus

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:12.810
of the packing party idea, was like,

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:14.950
when you move, you actually have to go through

00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:17.850
everything you own and do something with it,

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:19.250
whether it's store it, box it up,

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:20.998
whatever, it doesn't matter.

00:22:20.998 --> 00:22:22.380
You have to do something with that stuff.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:25.440
And now is sort of the second time where this has happened

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:26.660
where people have been in their homes

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:28.870
for way more than they anticipated.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:31.110
And we thought at the beginning of this

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:33.020
like people were reaching out to us like,

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:34.880
hey, you guys, you're probably upset

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:36.420
that you got rid of all that stuff, aren't you,

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:37.450
now that we're stuck at home?

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:39.630
And I'm like, yeah, the broken waffle iron

00:22:39.630 --> 00:22:42.740
is really, I'm missing that right now.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:45.660
The third bread maker that I got as a wedding gift.

00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:48.440
And so anyway that packing party,

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:50.600
we've had dozens of people do it.

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:52.390
In fact, we have a book coming out next year,

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:53.700
called Love People, Use Things.

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:57.720
It's like the whole sort of synopsis of our message.

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:00.340
And in that book we had 47 different families

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:01.450
do a packing party.

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:05.210
So it may seem radical, but it's not so radical

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.490
that, I mean, we've had a lot of people do it.

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:09.217
The idea being, you pack everything up

00:23:09.217 --> 00:23:11.420
and then you slowly unpack

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:14.060
and deliberately decide which items

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:17.050
you actually need to use

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:18.780
and the rest gets donated or sold.

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:20.307
Right, yeah, I mean, Josh had asked me, he's like,

00:23:20.307 --> 00:23:22.207
"What if you unpacked things as you needed it?

00:23:22.207 --> 00:23:23.861
"that would really help."

00:23:23.861 --> 00:23:24.694
And I'm like, "Yeah, that's a great idea.

00:23:24.694 --> 00:23:25.610
"That's what we should do."

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:27.870
And so you can imagine, yeah, my clothes for work,

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:30.850
bed and bed sheets, toothbrush, so forth and so on.

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:33.490
But I'll tell you, the packing party,

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:36.280
it was something to like change my state.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.680
But I honestly didn't realize how powerful

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:41.120
that was going to be until it was over

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:43.370
and I was confronted with all my things.

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:47.910
And the biggest revelation was I had this dream

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:48.760
of retiring early,

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:52.973
but I had like very little in my retirement accounts.

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:56.540
But here I saw tens of thousands of dollars

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:59.830
that I wasn't using worth of stuff.

00:23:59.830 --> 00:24:01.340
And I'm like, hmm, that could probably

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:03.650
be sitting in my retirement account right now.

00:24:03.650 --> 00:24:05.637
But I remember going to Josh, I'm like,

00:24:05.637 --> 00:24:08.147
"Dude, we have to do a website

00:24:08.147 --> 00:24:10.517
"and talk about this packing party.

00:24:10.517 --> 00:24:13.350
"'Cause this is something."

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:15.770
So that was the original impetus to start the blog.

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:16.690
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:19.000
It started 10 years ago yesterday.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:19.833
I know.

00:24:19.833 --> 00:24:21.385
I saw an Instagram, your 10 year,

00:24:21.385 --> 00:24:23.327
you guys have been doing this for 10 years.

00:24:23.327 --> 00:24:24.529
It's wild.
10 years, yeah.

00:24:24.529 --> 00:24:25.362
That's unbelievable, man.

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:31.300
What's the difference between your relationship

00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:33.800
to minimalism then in the early days to now?

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:36.423
Like how has it evolved or changed?

00:24:37.450 --> 00:24:40.010
I think it's changed quite a bit recently.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:41.430
I've become more allergic

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:44.270
to the sort of how to side of things.

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:46.970
I've never been a giant fan of it,

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:49.700
but I've realized that it's actually,

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:50.930
before I was somewhat neutral on it.

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:51.870
It just wasn't for me.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:55.940
But now I think it's often a problem.

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:59.890
And I think it's an opiate in a way because it helps,

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:02.810
as Ryan alluded to earlier, the sort of the symptoms.

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:05.600
Like if I show you how to declutter your kitchen

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:06.570
but you don't know why you're doing it,

00:25:06.570 --> 00:25:08.770
it's gonna be re cluttered a month from now.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:11.430
And so getting a deep understanding,

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:14.600
if you understand the why, truly understand the why,

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.550
the how will take care of itself.

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:17.900
And so we'll talk about some

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:19.670
of the how stuff that we've done,

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:22.146
that may not be applicable for everyone, by the way.

00:25:22.146 --> 00:25:25.340
My why looks different from Ryan's why

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.330
and that's why starting out with that question,

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:28.860
how might your life be better with less

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:31.290
is really the the foundation of it.

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:32.730
Then we can talk about the how to.

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:33.930
But it's the process,

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:37.210
it's the how that opens the portal to the why.

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:38.810
Like if you're living your life

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:40.690
in a certain way with blinders on

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:43.780
and just moving in a particular direction,

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:46.460
it's very hard to answer that question of why.

00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:49.050
You got to shake things up and do something different

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:51.773
in order to kind of confront that.

00:25:52.706 --> 00:25:54.440
Short of you doing the packing party,

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:57.790
would you have been, if confronted with the why question,

00:25:57.790 --> 00:25:58.900
how are you gonna answer that?

00:25:58.900 --> 00:26:02.490
So it's almost like there needs to be

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:06.710
a deconstruction of your life a little bit

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:08.613
and there's some practicalities that

00:26:08.613 --> 00:26:09.446
get baked into that.
I'm all for that

00:26:09.446 --> 00:26:10.279
It's interesting.

00:26:12.213 --> 00:26:17.213
It's almost like the why was so much

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:22.370
in my face at the time of how miserable I was.

00:26:22.370 --> 00:26:23.560
It was like

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:24.993
But also you're holding onto it so hard

00:26:24.993 --> 00:26:26.440
because your whole life

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:28.080
was invested in that.
Oh 100%.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:33.080
Yeah, but if it wasn't for that amount of,

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:37.820
or that level of stress and discontent and depression,

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:39.260
I don't know if I would have.

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:40.093
'Cause the problem is sometimes.

00:26:40.093 --> 00:26:41.240
Pain is the lever.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.283
Yeah and comfort is like,

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:47.090
I don't know, comfort to me is,

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:48.990
don't get me wrong, I like being comfortable, but

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:50.480
It's fuel for denial.

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:52.120
Absolutely.

00:26:52.120 --> 00:26:53.700
The thing I look at with what Ryan

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:55.500
I think Ryan is actually the perfect example

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.140
of if you understand the why,

00:26:57.140 --> 00:26:58.220
the how it takes care of itself.

00:26:58.220 --> 00:27:00.140
No one gave him a how to,

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:03.320
there was not a prescription for a packing party ever.

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:05.110
It didn't exist.

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:06.680
What did exist were a bunch

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:08.290
of decluttering tips and other things

00:27:08.290 --> 00:27:09.680
that we had seen for decades

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:10.890
and never paid any attention to,

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:13.200
because that's not that compelling.

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:16.140
It's compelling in the moment at the checkout line,

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:19.530
the same way candy is appealing at the checkout line.

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:21.520
But it's not nutritious in any way.

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:24.110
And so when Ryan truly understood the why,

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:27.473
like, oh, I don't feel at peace, I feel chaos,

00:27:28.496 --> 00:27:32.230
let me sort through this.

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:35.110
Let me not fix it because nothing is fixed.

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:36.280
There's always change.

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:38.240
It's not about fixing something,

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.230
it's about addressing the problem.

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:42.390
I think that's what the packing party did.

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:43.223
It was the impetus.

00:27:43.223 --> 00:27:45.310
Now, yeah, we talk about the how,

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:47.770
but Ryan always talk about how what we share

00:27:47.770 --> 00:27:50.380
is like a recipe and you can sort of tweeze out

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:52.640
your own ingredients and create your own version of it,

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:54.230
adjust for taste.

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:57.070
So how long after this packing party

00:27:57.070 --> 00:28:00.850
and this decision to write about these things

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:03.300
before you guys are no longer working

00:28:03.300 --> 00:28:04.480
at the corporation?

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:05.830
Like I know you got laid off.

00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:08.620
I got laid off, yeah, months after Josh.

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:10.950
Josh technically got laid off too.

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:12.555
I laid myself off.

00:28:12.555 --> 00:28:13.530
He did.

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:15.320
It was, well, they came to me.

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:16.987
So this was 10 years ago as well.

00:28:16.987 --> 00:28:19.030
And they came to me right before Christmas said,

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:23.170
as soon as we finish out the holiday shopping season,

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:24.003
which by the way,

00:28:24.003 --> 00:28:26.580
we've turned the holiday season into a shopping season.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:28.080
Think about that for a moment.

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:31.380
But they said, as soon as we do this,

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:36.120
I need you to close eight stores and lay off 42 employees.

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:38.109
I had done this a bunch of times.

00:28:38.109 --> 00:28:40.870
It was never fun, but it got easier over time.

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:44.120
So I laid off a bunch of people and fired people.

00:28:44.120 --> 00:28:45.610
It wasn't that big of a deal.

00:28:45.610 --> 00:28:47.740
But then with this new perspective

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:50.933
I had been simplifying my life that entire year.

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:55.410
They said you got two weeks to put together this plan,

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:58.700
give us the 42 names that you're going to lay off.

00:28:58.700 --> 00:29:00.860
And so I went home and within two days

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:02.710
I was putting the spreadsheets together.

00:29:02.710 --> 00:29:05.800
But looking at the spreadsheet was like looking

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:09.830
at a rainbow in black and white and gray scale.

00:29:09.830 --> 00:29:12.670
Because these were just names and statistics.

00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:14.350
And I get that's how you're going to

00:29:14.350 --> 00:29:16.060
make a decision like this.

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:17.120
But these weren't just names.

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:20.920
These were husbands and fathers and daughters and mothers.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:23.600
And these were people.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:26.450
And you know, my livelihood was in their hands.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:29.100
And I knew that like there was some sort of discontent

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:30.820
but at that point I realized the corporation

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:34.560
we were at no longer aligned with my personal values.

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.500
And so I turned the plan in,

00:29:36.500 --> 00:29:38.470
my name was the first name on the list.

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:40.770
So you really did lay yourself off.

00:29:40.770 --> 00:29:41.603
Yeah.

00:29:41.603 --> 00:29:42.436
Wow.

00:29:42.436 --> 00:29:45.100
And I didn't really, I didn't have a plan.

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:47.980
I just had reduced my bills so significantly

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:51.510
living in Dayton Ohio, $500 a month apartment.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:52.680
And I was like, you know what?

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.470
I'm gonna try this writing thing out for a while.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:57.143
There was a coffee shop in my neighborhood,

00:29:57.143 --> 00:29:58.050
they actually ended up in the documentary.

00:29:58.050 --> 00:29:58.883
Press.

00:29:58.883 --> 00:29:59.716
Press, yeah.

00:29:59.716 --> 00:30:01.520
And I said, I'll just work there

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:03.120
and I'll have enough money to pay my bills.

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:05.530
And I'll write for the rest of the time.

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:07.290
I wanted to write fiction initially.

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:09.390
Thanks to Ryan, this was a beautiful accident

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:11.370
in a different direction.

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:13.330
So you guys start meeting at Press,

00:30:13.330 --> 00:30:15.123
you start working on this blog.

00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:18.700
I mean, that's what we did for the first year.

00:30:18.700 --> 00:30:21.890
We just wrote on the blog and yeah,

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:23.587
put our thoughts out there.

00:30:23.587 --> 00:30:26.400
Was there a moment, like a tipping point moment

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:29.320
with the blog where you're like, wow,

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:30.930
this could be a thing.

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:33.380
We could actually craft a vocation around this?

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:34.213
What is the tipping point.

00:30:34.213 --> 00:30:36.738
I think for me, it's the San Francisco event.

00:30:36.738 --> 00:30:38.112
For me, it was before that.

00:30:38.112 --> 00:30:38.945
It was before that?

00:30:38.945 --> 00:30:41.530
Yeah, for me it was Leo Babauta

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:42.980
shared something that we did.

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:47.430
I was reading his blog back then too.

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:51.314
That was like, everyone was reading his stuff.

00:30:51.314 --> 00:30:52.766
And we had like more people show up in one day.

00:30:52.766 --> 00:30:53.908
And it wasn't a lot of people,

00:30:53.908 --> 00:30:55.250
it was like 9,000 people or something.

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:57.891
For me, it was like, oh, we haven't seen,

00:30:57.891 --> 00:30:58.800
so we talk about this in the film,

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:02.090
how like 52 readers that first month turned into 500,

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:04.170
which turned into 5 million or whatever.

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:08.870
And so there was a moment there,

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:10.680
but around that same time, it was like,

00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:12.030
hey, you guys are The Minimalists, aren't you?

00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:13.610
Did you write a book about minimalism?

00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:15.600
And it's like, oh, I guess we should do that, huh?

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.030
We are the minimalists.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.560
And so we did, we put out a book.

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:21.500
At the time we just self published a book

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:23.130
at the end of our first year.

00:31:23.130 --> 00:31:25.520
And we went on tour, but it was really just,

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:27.635
we went around the country to coffee shops.

00:31:27.635 --> 00:31:30.000
And it made me realize like,

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.840
oh, if we can get eight people to show up

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:33.673
and buy a book.

00:31:33.673 --> 00:31:36.030
Most stops were like two to eight people.

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:36.960
Yeah.

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:38.440
If we sell 10 books

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:40.060
When you're living minimally,

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:41.218
you don't need much.

00:31:41.218 --> 00:31:42.051
No exactly.

00:31:43.086 --> 00:31:44.560
If we could sell 10 books.

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:46.060
Then we have a place to stay for the night.

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:49.060
If not, then we'd just sleep in Ryan's Toyota Corolla.

00:31:49.060 --> 00:31:51.050
And that's how it worked out for us.

00:31:51.050 --> 00:31:53.190
Well, the first movie did such a great job

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:56.630
at spinning that yarn of you guys going on the road

00:31:56.630 --> 00:31:58.310
and showing up at places

00:31:58.310 --> 00:31:59.730
and three people would show up.

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:01.847
And then slowly a few more, a few more,

00:32:01.847 --> 00:32:03.620
and you see the build.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:06.750
But it's the grittiness of the early days

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:09.419
that was so fun.

00:32:09.419 --> 00:32:11.130
I'm just thinking about that South by event

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:12.170
in that minimalism documentary

00:32:12.170 --> 00:32:13.470
because I remember being so excited

00:32:13.470 --> 00:32:16.123
for that South by Southwest event, like, we made it.

00:32:18.331 --> 00:32:20.312
We finally, we finally.

00:32:20.312 --> 00:32:22.057
It's a buddy movie road trip on

00:32:22.057 --> 00:32:25.550
like a wizard of Oz movie with South by Southwest

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:27.490
being you guys going to meet Oz.

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:31.310
And I was thinking, this was like the apex of our journey.

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:33.220
Like we were at South by Southwest,

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:36.170
this is, and then like, yeah, three people showed up to it.

00:32:37.719 --> 00:32:39.283
And they're like on their phones,

00:32:39.283 --> 00:32:42.060
by the way, it's 8:00 a.m. on a Sunday morning,

00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:44.772
Ryan is trying to talk about living with less.

00:32:44.772 --> 00:32:45.613
I'm like, hi everybody.

00:32:45.613 --> 00:32:46.446
Yeah, that was great.

00:32:46.446 --> 00:32:47.279
Lanyards.

00:32:47.279 --> 00:32:48.919
This big room with like three people in it.

00:32:48.919 --> 00:32:49.752
Well, that movie is so well done.

00:32:49.752 --> 00:32:53.540
And I have to imagine that that had to be

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:54.870
life-changing in some regard.

00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:57.320
I think it's most people's introduction to your work

00:32:57.320 --> 00:32:59.540
because Netflix put it right out front.

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:02.040
And so many people watched it.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:04.450
The first question I have about that first movie

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:08.305
is how did you connect with Matt D'Avella?

00:33:08.305 --> 00:33:11.940
'Cause Matt D'Avella, he's had his own crazy trajectory

00:33:11.940 --> 00:33:13.160
blowing up on YouTube

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:16.328
and being this massive creator on that platform.

00:33:16.328 --> 00:33:17.570
100%.

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:18.403
He's all grown up.

00:33:18.403 --> 00:33:20.760
We connected with him on Twitter

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:23.430
when he was a wedding videographer.

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.430
And I saw this video he made

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:27.440
called Most Wedding Videographers Suck.

00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:30.200
And I still bring that up to him constantly

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:33.360
but he was doing that

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:35.090
and he was doing like other commercial work

00:33:35.090 --> 00:33:37.350
for like Toyota or Subaru or something.

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:41.230
And we hired him to do the trailer

00:33:41.230 --> 00:33:44.580
for our second book, Everything That Remains.

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:46.120
And we did an event out in New York

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:48.120
to sort of celebrate this.

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:51.060
And he filmed that event and made a trailer

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:53.830
for that book that we put up on YouTube.

00:33:53.830 --> 00:33:54.710
And that was sort of it.

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:56.407
And then a few months later, I said,

00:33:56.407 --> 00:33:58.130
"Hey, we're getting ready to go on the road."

00:33:58.130 --> 00:33:59.790
2014, we did a hundred city tour.

00:33:59.790 --> 00:34:01.257
And said, "Hey, do you want to come

00:34:01.257 --> 00:34:03.609
"on the road with us and try to make a documentary?"

00:34:03.609 --> 00:34:06.090
And he had been wanting to do something

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:08.850
with his creative skills other than commercial work.

00:34:08.850 --> 00:34:11.570
And so he's like, "Yeah, I'll give it a shot."

00:34:11.570 --> 00:34:13.270
At the end of the tour it was like,

00:34:13.270 --> 00:34:15.580
he just had a few hard drives

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:17.780
with like a thousand hours of footage.

00:34:17.780 --> 00:34:19.643
It was like, hey, good luck with that.

00:34:20.756 --> 00:34:22.360
So he just went away and came back

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:23.960
with a cut for you guys?

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:24.793
Yeah.

00:34:24.793 --> 00:34:26.290
I remember dropping him off at the airport

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:28.263
and I just remember thinking to myself,

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:30.600
I don't think this is a movie.

00:34:31.694 --> 00:34:34.610
I'm like, I have no idea how he's gonna pull this together.

00:34:34.610 --> 00:34:36.900
There was no budget for it.

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:38.780
I mean, we were just passionate about it,

00:34:38.780 --> 00:34:42.000
so it's not like we had a lot to lose

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:43.900
but then he sent that first cut,

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:47.960
and yeah, I was instantly like, oh wow.

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:49.140
Matt knows what he's doing.

00:34:49.140 --> 00:34:50.480
And I always make sure and tell people that

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:51.757
because people will come up to me and they'll be like,

00:34:51.757 --> 00:34:53.677
"Oh, you're the guy from the minimalism documentary.

00:34:53.677 --> 00:34:55.038
"Great doc.

00:34:55.038 --> 00:34:56.042
"You did such a good job."

00:34:56.042 --> 00:34:56.875
And I'm like, I wish I could say it was me

00:34:56.875 --> 00:34:58.610
but it's really Matt D'Avella

00:34:58.610 --> 00:35:00.715
who did that whole documentary.

00:35:00.715 --> 00:35:01.680
He did an unbelievable job

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:05.590
and all the accolades and success that he's he's enjoying

00:35:05.590 --> 00:35:07.250
on YouTube are well-deserved.

00:35:07.250 --> 00:35:08.557
Oh yeah,
Heck yeah.

00:35:08.557 --> 00:35:09.835
What he does is amazing on YouTube.

00:35:09.835 --> 00:35:13.790
And he has built up this amazing following,

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:17.020
especially if young people, who really,

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:18.720
he's brought the message of minimalism

00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:21.810
to a crowd that that maybe Ryan and I

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:23.575
weren't ever going to reach either.

00:35:23.575 --> 00:35:24.686
Yeah absolutely.

00:35:24.686 --> 00:35:27.550
He makes these like mini documentaries.

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:30.500
I know every one of his videos is like,

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:32.513
should enjoy a theatrical release.

00:35:33.827 --> 00:35:37.026
I'm going to tell him that compliment

00:35:37.026 --> 00:35:38.210
next time I see him.
Yeah, and he's a guy

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:43.210
who walks the talk, like he just this past week

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:45.702
gave away all his stuff yet again.

00:35:45.702 --> 00:35:49.310
And him and his wife are on the road and now in Sydney,

00:35:49.310 --> 00:35:50.420
like they're just gonna travel.

00:35:50.420 --> 00:35:51.650
They literally, everything they own

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:54.470
is in like little carry on suitcases.

00:35:54.470 --> 00:35:58.440
It's a beautiful example of how you can

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:00.320
turn your life around on a dime

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:02.560
when you have the power to let go

00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:04.220
of anything in your life.

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:07.858
And I mean, that's exactly what he displayed with that.

00:36:07.858 --> 00:36:10.870
'Cause minimalism is not about owning nothing.

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:12.330
It's just about breaking free

00:36:12.330 --> 00:36:15.386
from that attachment, breaking free from that,

00:36:15.386 --> 00:36:19.495
I don't know another word for it.

00:36:19.495 --> 00:36:20.570
Just that attraction that we all have for stuff.

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:21.690
There's a sense that, oh,

00:36:21.690 --> 00:36:23.720
if you're going to be a minimalist,

00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:27.010
that you're self-flagellating

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:28.700
or you're some kind of martyr,

00:36:28.700 --> 00:36:31.610
when in truth you're just purchasing freedom for yourself.

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:32.830
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:36:32.830 --> 00:36:34.500
And choices.

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:37.800
To be able to, you know, self-employment is such a gift,

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:40.150
but it gives him with the things that he does,

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:45.150
the ability to just pack it up and go wherever he wants,

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:47.200
which is unbelievable.

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:49.110
And when you see him work, it's amazing.

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:51.210
You wouldn't think he's able to,

00:36:51.210 --> 00:36:53.530
he has the strangest sort of,

00:36:53.530 --> 00:36:55.700
he's like hiding under the table,

00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:57.440
looking up with the camera.

00:36:57.440 --> 00:36:58.930
You're like, what is he doing?

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:00.230
Is that a crotch shot?

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:01.330
I'm not sure what's going on here.

00:37:01.330 --> 00:37:02.850
But somehow he just

00:37:03.963 --> 00:37:04.850
And a total one man show.

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:07.930
When I did his, I was one of the early guests on his podcast

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:09.750
and he's got all these cameras,

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:11.930
and he's doing it all himself.

00:37:11.930 --> 00:37:13.970
He's manning the cameras, he's asking the questions.

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:14.950
He's got the monitor there.

00:37:14.950 --> 00:37:17.360
I'm like, this looks stressful.

00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:18.680
Like, get a little help.

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:20.610
But he's like, no, he knows what he's doing.

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:22.990
He's like a master of his domain.

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:26.840
His hidden secret is his skill at editing.

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:31.260
So we had some help on this film,

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.402
Angus wall, I don't know if you know Angus wall,

00:37:33.402 --> 00:37:34.564
Uh uh.

00:37:34.564 --> 00:37:35.900
But he's David Fincher's editor.

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:37.750
Oh wow.
So he edited Fight club,

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:41.188
he edited The Social Network.

00:37:41.188 --> 00:37:43.670
He won some Academy award for that.

00:37:43.670 --> 00:37:45.263
He edited, what's the one with,

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:47.960
what's the other big one he did?

00:37:49.300 --> 00:37:51.460
I know it's on the tip of my tongue too.

00:37:51.460 --> 00:37:52.870
Girl With The Dragon Tattoo.

00:37:52.870 --> 00:37:54.480
He did the opening sequence to that.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.920
Anyway, his company did the animation in Less Is Now.

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:01.057
And he was actually, we were gonna bring him

00:38:01.057 --> 00:38:03.530
and that team on to edit the film.

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:05.463
But Matt was just like, "No, I'm good.

00:38:06.307 --> 00:38:07.510
"I got it covered."

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:08.560
And that's the thing,

00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:09.990
the weird thing about it is I don't think

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:11.480
Matt likes editing at all

00:38:12.410 --> 00:38:14.120
but that is his hidden,

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:17.090
he's the Mozart of editing.

00:38:17.090 --> 00:38:22.090
And he, even when we went from fourth cut to fifth cut,

00:38:22.190 --> 00:38:24.197
Angus saw it and he was like,

00:38:24.197 --> 00:38:25.777
"This looks like it went through 60 different

00:38:25.777 --> 00:38:27.475
"cuts of the film."

00:38:27.475 --> 00:38:29.680
Yeah, kudos to him.

00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:32.260
So the movie explodes

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:35.410
and you guys kind of ride this wave.

00:38:35.410 --> 00:38:39.410
You have the books and you started the podcast.

00:38:39.410 --> 00:38:40.770
You're now 10 years into this thing.

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:42.560
You've got millions of people all over the world

00:38:42.560 --> 00:38:45.180
that care deeply about the things that you talk about.

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:48.030
And what's really cool and compelling

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:52.090
about this platform that you now manage

00:38:52.090 --> 00:38:55.060
is that it's completely audience supported.

00:38:55.060 --> 00:38:58.210
You start all your podcasts, advertisements suck.

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:00.630
And you have this population of people

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:03.157
that adore you and basically

00:39:03.157 --> 00:39:07.240
are willing to subscribe to your wisdom.

00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:10.060
And you've been able to craft out

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:12.800
making a living off of this for a decade.

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:16.083
Most of what we do is free and available to the public.

00:39:17.140 --> 00:39:20.420
And there's a small portion where we sort of dive deeper.

00:39:20.420 --> 00:39:23.690
And our private podcast is on Patreon.

00:39:23.690 --> 00:39:26.430
And yeah, we do have an audience that supports us there

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:29.000
but that's also, it's almost like when you go to,

00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:31.180
well, back when we could go to the Comedy Store

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:32.013
or places like that

00:39:32.013 --> 00:39:34.220
where people sort of work out ideas.

00:39:34.220 --> 00:39:35.510
We work out things.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:37.470
And sometimes Ryan and I just argue

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:39.510
on the private podcast about things

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:41.710
because we don't agree about something

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:44.970
or we bring a guest on and we argue with them about it.

00:39:44.970 --> 00:39:47.663
I like to think of it as talking things out.

00:39:47.663 --> 00:39:49.996
(all laugh)

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:54.000
But most of what we do is,

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:58.493
whether it's the blog or the main podcast,

00:39:59.837 --> 00:40:02.160
it's accessible to anyone.

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.470
When you were touring, how many cities did you do?

00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:06.600
I mean, you guys invited me to come

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:08.410
when you did the Fillmore in San Francisco,

00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:10.030
which was like, so cool, man.

00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:13.150
You guys were filling theaters all across the country.

00:40:13.150 --> 00:40:14.587
Yeah, you were at the Fillmore with us.

00:40:14.587 --> 00:40:15.559
Super fun.

00:40:15.559 --> 00:40:16.840
That was great.

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:18.240
I mean the most we've ever done was 100 cities,

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:21.370
119 events in one year.

00:40:21.370 --> 00:40:22.203
Wow.

00:40:22.203 --> 00:40:24.663
We canceled a tour last this year, 2020,

00:40:26.043 --> 00:40:27.975
because of 2020.

00:40:27.975 --> 00:40:30.220
For obvious reasons.

00:40:30.220 --> 00:40:34.040
And yeah, we've done what nine tours in 10 years.

00:40:34.040 --> 00:40:36.470
So anywhere from a few cities

00:40:36.470 --> 00:40:39.043
like the Simply Southern Tour was three cities.

00:40:39.043 --> 00:40:39.920
We went with Dave Ramsey's team

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:42.460
and did a small tour in the South.

00:40:42.460 --> 00:40:44.580
To 100 cities everywhere in between.

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:46.750
But yeah, it just became a part of,

00:40:46.750 --> 00:40:48.450
and by the way, all those tours were different as well.

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:49.650
Sometimes it's a podcast tour.

00:40:49.650 --> 00:40:51.360
Sometimes we do a talk.

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:55.390
Sometimes we'll do book readings and book signings.

00:40:55.390 --> 00:40:57.610
It kind of just depends on what's going on.

00:40:57.610 --> 00:40:59.290
So Dave Ramsey features

00:40:59.290 --> 00:41:01.260
pretty prominently in the new movie.

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:03.310
So how did that relationship develop?

00:41:03.310 --> 00:41:06.690
How does he fit into the minimalist ecosystem?

00:41:06.690 --> 00:41:09.142
His daughter really likes us.

00:41:09.142 --> 00:41:09.975
Is that what it is?

00:41:09.975 --> 00:41:10.808
Yeah.

00:41:10.808 --> 00:41:11.641
His daughter works for him.

00:41:11.641 --> 00:41:12.770
She's a very talented author.

00:41:12.770 --> 00:41:15.560
Her name is Rachel Cruz and she has a great YouTube show.

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:18.070
And she invited us to come on her YouTube show.

00:41:18.070 --> 00:41:21.210
It's in Nashville and it's a whole production.

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:23.968
They have a whole studio set up there.

00:41:23.968 --> 00:41:25.660
He has 1,000 employees at his compound.

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:27.623
It's a really impressive setup.

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:33.840
And they work really hard to teach financial literacy.

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:37.560
They're now being taught in 25%

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:40.450
of the high schools around the country,

00:41:40.450 --> 00:41:42.050
the Dave Ramsey curriculum.

00:41:42.050 --> 00:41:44.310
He has a whole team, like 100 people on that team.

00:41:44.310 --> 00:41:48.310
His whole thing is about living fiscally responsible,

00:41:48.310 --> 00:41:49.850
like not living outside of your means

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:53.450
and making sure that you're making prudent financial,

00:41:53.450 --> 00:41:56.010
essentially conservative prudent financial disease.

00:41:56.010 --> 00:41:58.290
Yeah, I feel like he's like

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.080
a minimalist finance guy basically,

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:01.520
because you know, minimalism,

00:42:01.520 --> 00:42:04.410
it's funny when I first heard it

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:05.870
and heard about it and looked into it,

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:09.050
I kind of thought like it was this niche type thing.

00:42:09.050 --> 00:42:11.310
And then once I got into it, I'm like,

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:14.040
oh, this can be applied to anything.

00:42:14.040 --> 00:42:16.110
And I think Dave Ramsey essentially

00:42:16.110 --> 00:42:17.733
applies minimalism to finances.

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:21.687
He's got some great financial advice.

00:42:21.687 --> 00:42:22.520
His whole team though, they're great.

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:24.143
Anthony O'Neil, Chris Hogan.

00:42:25.102 --> 00:42:26.560
The whole lot of them.

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:27.393
John Delony

00:42:27.393 --> 00:42:28.735
Yeah, Ken.

00:42:28.735 --> 00:42:33.083
So we're well into this pandemic cycle,

00:42:34.582 --> 00:42:36.820
fraying at the edges
pandemic cycle

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:39.509
as we crawl towards 2021.

00:42:39.509 --> 00:42:41.480
And I'm interested in how you're thinking

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:44.160
about the relationship between consumerism

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:47.672
and minimalism with this very specific moment,

00:42:47.672 --> 00:42:50.080
the way that I'm kind of thinking

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:53.420
about it is that on the one hand,

00:42:53.420 --> 00:42:54.790
nobody's going to the mall,

00:42:54.790 --> 00:42:56.780
they're not shopping, they're at home.

00:42:56.780 --> 00:42:59.500
So it's forcing people to perhaps be a little bit

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:03.490
more reflective about those habits on some level,

00:43:03.490 --> 00:43:07.070
yet at the same time, we're all empowered by these tools.

00:43:07.070 --> 00:43:09.210
And we're spending, these technological tools,

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:12.580
we're all in front of our screens way too much.

00:43:12.580 --> 00:43:14.950
And that habituation to shopping

00:43:14.950 --> 00:43:18.130
has just migrated to the devices.

00:43:18.130 --> 00:43:20.700
I don't know what the statistics are but I suspect,

00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:24.150
I'm certain that online shopping has skyrocketed

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:28.390
but has consumerism overall increased during this moment?

00:43:28.390 --> 00:43:30.890
How are you thinking about people's habits?

00:43:30.890 --> 00:43:32.659
What does that look like right now?

00:43:32.659 --> 00:43:33.492
It depends what we mean by consumerism.

00:43:33.492 --> 00:43:35.930
So I do think consumerism is the right way to frame it.

00:43:35.930 --> 00:43:37.830
That is one of the problems.

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:38.960
Consumption isn't the problem.

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:41.360
We talk about this in Less Is Now

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:43.240
because we all need to consume some stuff.

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:46.670
Consumerism, which is what we could just identify

00:43:46.670 --> 00:43:49.010
as compulsory consumption in a way

00:43:49.010 --> 00:43:52.050
as though we feel that we must buy this

00:43:52.050 --> 00:43:53.100
in order to either be complete,

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:55.663
Ryan talked about the deficit advertising earlier,

00:43:56.620 --> 00:43:58.800
or the what else is Annie call it in the film?

00:43:58.800 --> 00:44:00.743
The vertical integration.

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:04.230
And so no longer are you just competing

00:44:04.230 --> 00:44:05.550
with your next door neighbor.

00:44:05.550 --> 00:44:07.220
Yeah, talk about that.

00:44:07.220 --> 00:44:09.770
That's a really fascinating, important idea, I think.

00:44:09.770 --> 00:44:10.630
So here's the new problem.

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:13.360
Consumerism is one problem when we relate that to stuff,

00:44:13.360 --> 00:44:15.590
materialism is really that,

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:18.860
the other side of consumerism is distraction, I think.

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:21.470
And so the thing that Ryan and I will say

00:44:21.470 --> 00:44:24.690
is scrolling is the new smoking.

00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:28.730
And this epiphany hit me when I was walking

00:44:28.730 --> 00:44:30.630
outside of like a Chipotle or something.

00:44:30.630 --> 00:44:32.220
I see someone outside in the cold,

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:34.640
like in the Midwest, scrolling on their phone,

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:36.730
cigarette in hand at the same time.

00:44:36.730 --> 00:44:38.320
And it's not to judge that person,

00:44:38.320 --> 00:44:40.290
it's to see myself in that person.

00:44:40.290 --> 00:44:42.287
I don't smoke, but I could see,

00:44:42.287 --> 00:44:44.070
my habits are showing.

00:44:44.070 --> 00:44:46.130
And it's not just the smoking.

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:47.900
Now, 50 years ago everyone

00:44:47.900 --> 00:44:49.840
sort of casually puffed cigarettes.

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:51.360
We'd be at this table right now,

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:53.337
just smoking, talking.

00:44:53.337 --> 00:44:55.156
It'd be a cloud.

00:44:55.156 --> 00:44:57.530
But you did that now, it would seem nutty.

00:44:57.530 --> 00:44:59.620
If I just lit up a cigarette right now,

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:01.850
you wouldn't know how to respond to that.

00:45:01.850 --> 00:45:03.390
The only person who could do that is Dave Chappelle,

00:45:03.390 --> 00:45:07.330
by the way, but besides him, it would seem nutty.

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:11.010
And yet it doesn't seem that strange if I,

00:45:11.010 --> 00:45:13.040
it would because of the three of us,

00:45:13.040 --> 00:45:14.380
but at your average setting

00:45:14.380 --> 00:45:15.340
if I were to pull out a phone

00:45:15.340 --> 00:45:16.890
and just check it really quick,

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:20.610
but it's just as bothersome in many ways.

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:22.640
It's secondhand distraction.

00:45:22.640 --> 00:45:25.080
And so I think over the last decade in particular,

00:45:25.080 --> 00:45:30.080
what we've seen is a lot of new ways to distract ourselves.

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:31.760
Ryan and I distracted ourselves back

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:36.750
in the aughties and in the late '90s with stuff.

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:38.560
But now the distractions are digital

00:45:38.560 --> 00:45:39.480
and they're right there.

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:42.300
And they're more enticing than ever.

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:43.730
The same thing happens.

00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:45.130
In the material world,

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:49.490
it was high paid demographers, statisticians,

00:45:49.490 --> 00:45:52.080
marketers, aggregating your eyeballs

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:54.070
onto their product or service.

00:45:54.070 --> 00:45:56.670
Now, since you're the product,

00:45:56.670 --> 00:45:58.507
it's aggregating your eyeballs onto the product

00:45:58.507 --> 00:46:01.560
and service to sell you products and services.

00:46:01.560 --> 00:46:04.610
And so in a weird way, a dystopian way,

00:46:04.610 --> 00:46:06.440
it elevates the problem.

00:46:06.440 --> 00:46:08.590
It amplifies the problem.

00:46:08.590 --> 00:46:10.120
I mean, without a doubt,

00:46:10.120 --> 00:46:12.050
and this is subject matter and terrain

00:46:12.050 --> 00:46:15.500
that movies like the Social Dilemma go into at length.

00:46:15.500 --> 00:46:18.620
It's not just enticing, it's truly addictive.

00:46:18.620 --> 00:46:20.780
And it's designed to be that way.

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:24.800
The revelatory idea that I had not thought about before

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:27.470
until I saw your movie was this idea

00:46:27.470 --> 00:46:30.560
of the kind of exponential expansion

00:46:30.560 --> 00:46:32.210
of keeping up with the Joneses,

00:46:32.210 --> 00:46:37.210
this consumerist impulse originally derived

00:46:37.320 --> 00:46:39.670
from perhaps something genetic within us

00:46:39.670 --> 00:46:42.370
to try to keep pace with our neighbors.

00:46:42.370 --> 00:46:44.830
So-and-so's got that refrigerator or that car,

00:46:44.830 --> 00:46:46.330
I've got to get that too.

00:46:46.330 --> 00:46:49.510
But now by dint of these technological tools,

00:46:49.510 --> 00:46:50.780
everybody's our neighbor.

00:46:50.780 --> 00:46:53.310
So it's not just the person living next door,

00:46:53.310 --> 00:46:55.427
but it's the celebrity on Instagram

00:46:55.427 --> 00:46:57.690
and you get to peek into their world,

00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:00.030
or now on Zoom, you get to see

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:03.130
what everybody's study and living room looks like.

00:47:03.130 --> 00:47:06.620
And it's almost impossible to not run that comparison

00:47:06.620 --> 00:47:09.277
against what you have or don't have.

00:47:09.277 --> 00:47:13.360
And how does that impact your consumer choices

00:47:13.360 --> 00:47:16.830
or your sense of innate discontent

00:47:16.830 --> 00:47:19.070
when you attempt to measure yourself

00:47:19.070 --> 00:47:20.973
against that impossible standard?

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:24.550
Yeah, I think comparison is the killer of joy,

00:47:24.550 --> 00:47:27.680
regardless of how you're comparing yourself

00:47:27.680 --> 00:47:29.270
or what you have to other people.

00:47:29.270 --> 00:47:31.350
It is interesting, like with the whole social media,

00:47:31.350 --> 00:47:34.090
it's like, my sister,

00:47:34.090 --> 00:47:36.340
when I was in Ohio a year ago,

00:47:36.340 --> 00:47:39.610
you know, back when we were traveling without any fear.

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:40.747
She was like, "Do you feel cool,

00:47:40.747 --> 00:47:42.738
"'cause you got, you know, X amount

00:47:42.738 --> 00:47:44.114
"of subscribers on Instagram?"

00:47:44.114 --> 00:47:44.947
And I'm like, "No."

00:47:44.947 --> 00:47:46.217
I was like, "Do you think I'm cool

00:47:46.217 --> 00:47:48.110
"because I have that many subscribers?"

00:47:48.110 --> 00:47:49.950
And she's like, "Yeah, I think it's pretty cool."

00:47:49.950 --> 00:47:52.976
And I'm like, "If you're looking at subscribers,

00:47:52.976 --> 00:47:54.087
"I don't even know what I have.

00:47:54.087 --> 00:47:56.647
"I know it's not a million", but I'm like,

00:47:56.647 --> 00:47:59.157
"If you got to where I'm at

00:47:59.157 --> 00:48:00.786
"with however many subscribers,

00:48:00.786 --> 00:48:02.257
"then you'd be looking at a million,

00:48:02.257 --> 00:48:03.547
"and then once you had a million

00:48:03.547 --> 00:48:05.250
"you'd be looking at 3 million."

00:48:05.250 --> 00:48:09.080
It's like a never ending comparison wheel

00:48:09.080 --> 00:48:11.040
that we put ourselves on.

00:48:11.040 --> 00:48:11.873
It's failure.

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:14.893
In a weird way, all success is failure.

00:48:15.880 --> 00:48:18.260
So what are the tactics that you deploy

00:48:18.260 --> 00:48:21.690
to prevent yourself from indulging

00:48:21.690 --> 00:48:25.640
in that kind of fruitless mental exercise.

00:48:25.640 --> 00:48:26.680
'Cause it's hard, right?

00:48:26.680 --> 00:48:27.513
Yeah, it is.

00:48:27.513 --> 00:48:31.730
I mean, I don't look at specific numbers.

00:48:31.730 --> 00:48:32.720
That's one way.

00:48:32.720 --> 00:48:36.560
Because I think, I'm a numbers guy in general.

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:38.180
I love math, I love spreadsheets.

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:40.490
So I could very easily get wrapped up into it

00:48:40.490 --> 00:48:42.170
just to like make a game out of it.

00:48:42.170 --> 00:48:45.480
But I try to not gamify it as much as possible

00:48:45.480 --> 00:48:47.673
but I'll be honest, because of,

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:50.341
man, this is gonna sound like some

00:48:50.341 --> 00:48:52.050
Buddhist hybrid thing,

00:48:52.050 --> 00:48:54.720
but because of how destructive

00:48:54.720 --> 00:48:56.880
my ego was in the corporate world,

00:48:56.880 --> 00:49:00.550
anytime I start to feel the ego kicking in,

00:49:00.550 --> 00:49:01.990
which it kicks in all the time,

00:49:01.990 --> 00:49:03.750
but when I start to feel it kicking in

00:49:03.750 --> 00:49:06.300
with like, oh, how many subscribers that I gained today?

00:49:06.300 --> 00:49:07.816
Or how many?

00:49:07.816 --> 00:49:08.930
'Cause I did feel that in the early days,

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:13.040
I try not to stroke that ego.

00:49:13.040 --> 00:49:15.720
And by practicing that over the last 10 years,

00:49:15.720 --> 00:49:18.080
I am able to just kind of still feel

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:19.150
like I felt 10 years ago.

00:49:19.150 --> 00:49:22.230
I don't feel any more popular or successful.

00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:23.310
I mean, even, it's funny,

00:49:23.310 --> 00:49:25.196
you were talking about us

00:49:25.196 --> 00:49:27.140
having a lot of people who adore us,

00:49:27.140 --> 00:49:28.060
I think is what you said,

00:49:28.060 --> 00:49:29.970
and they're willing to support us.

00:49:29.970 --> 00:49:31.640
And I appreciate those people so much

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:32.860
but like hearing you say it,

00:49:32.860 --> 00:49:35.250
my ego is like, oh wow, that is amazing.

00:49:35.250 --> 00:49:37.440
We do have all these people who adore us

00:49:37.440 --> 00:49:39.123
and who will support us.

00:49:40.732 --> 00:49:43.130
So I guess just to reiterate,

00:49:43.130 --> 00:49:45.950
when my ego starts to get out of control

00:49:45.950 --> 00:49:47.870
I will try to like put it in check a little bit

00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:49.930
because ultimately,

00:49:49.930 --> 00:49:51.480
I know if I ever start to play that game,

00:49:51.480 --> 00:49:53.370
it'll never be enough.

00:49:53.370 --> 00:49:54.306
Yeah.

00:49:54.306 --> 00:49:55.139
I don't have any tactics.

00:49:55.139 --> 00:49:56.990
You guys aren't getting into Twitter spats

00:49:56.990 --> 00:49:58.280
and things like that.

00:49:58.280 --> 00:50:01.020
You seem to just, I can tell by the way

00:50:01.020 --> 00:50:03.680
that you share your content online

00:50:03.680 --> 00:50:05.500
that it's kind of at an arms length.

00:50:05.500 --> 00:50:07.840
You make sure that your stuff is being shared

00:50:07.840 --> 00:50:11.380
but you're not in there

00:50:11.380 --> 00:50:13.680
going back and forth with people and stuff like that

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:14.917
A healthy detachment.

00:50:14.917 --> 00:50:17.490
I'm not the Buddha.

00:50:17.490 --> 00:50:19.940
The Buddha would be non-attachment right.

00:50:19.940 --> 00:50:22.320
But not needing the outcome

00:50:26.670 --> 00:50:29.193
and not needing any of it, by the way.

00:50:31.660 --> 00:50:35.613
By being attached detached you realize that,

00:50:39.010 --> 00:50:41.233
this is gonna sound like a value judgment,

00:50:41.233 --> 00:50:42.733
but it's not, it's all a scam, man.

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:46.060
Who cares?

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:47.380
It's all ego.

00:50:47.380 --> 00:50:51.510
Even by the way, what have I changed my mind about recently?

00:50:51.510 --> 00:50:53.264
Helping people.

00:50:53.264 --> 00:50:54.310
I don't want to help people.

00:50:54.310 --> 00:50:57.130
Let's be honest, helping people,

00:50:57.130 --> 00:50:59.930
that's just my ego saying I want to help people.

00:50:59.930 --> 00:51:01.780
Now, I feel it's still viscerally

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:03.170
because I've programmed myself.

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:04.690
You will be externally validated

00:51:04.690 --> 00:51:06.190
if you say that out loud

00:51:06.190 --> 00:51:09.448
Right, right, it's my, no, no, no, no, no.

00:51:09.448 --> 00:51:13.470
But maybe the truth behind that is I want peace.

00:51:13.470 --> 00:51:16.270
I want to speak the truth.

00:51:16.270 --> 00:51:19.980
And if that helps some people, that's wonderful.

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:22.530
If it doesn't, it can still be wonderful.

00:51:22.530 --> 00:51:23.890
I don't need that.

00:51:23.890 --> 00:51:25.920
Because once I become attached

00:51:25.920 --> 00:51:29.250
to needing to help someone, it's a different type of prison.

00:51:29.250 --> 00:51:31.040
It's a well decorated prison cell

00:51:31.040 --> 00:51:33.000
but it's still a prison cell.

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:35.768
You can help people for selfish reasons though too,

00:51:35.768 --> 00:51:36.601
'cause it makes you feel good.

00:51:36.601 --> 00:51:37.590
It actually makes you

00:51:38.444 --> 00:51:39.825
100%.

00:51:39.825 --> 00:51:40.658
It gives your life a little bit more meaning

00:51:41.984 --> 00:51:43.047
and it it builds self-esteem.

00:51:43.047 --> 00:51:45.410
So even if your impetus, your impulse is selfish,

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:47.810
it's still a good thing to follow through on.

00:51:47.810 --> 00:51:48.916
By the way,

00:51:48.916 --> 00:51:51.693
I'm not saying that helping people is good or bad.

00:51:55.570 --> 00:51:56.710
And also not helping people

00:51:56.710 --> 00:51:59.033
is not good or bad by default then.

00:52:00.260 --> 00:52:02.390
I don't want to throw that judgment out there.

00:52:02.390 --> 00:52:05.160
I'm simply saying that I have to be honest with myself

00:52:05.160 --> 00:52:07.550
that it's my ego that's involved

00:52:07.550 --> 00:52:10.260
or where I'm like, it is what Ryan said.

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:13.230
If I have 10 million Instagram followers,

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:14.510
I'm helping more people.

00:52:14.510 --> 00:52:18.943
Well, is that the truth or is that just a statistic?

00:52:20.400 --> 00:52:22.920
I feel like there's two interesting things

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:24.260
happening right now,

00:52:24.260 --> 00:52:29.260
which is the undeniable rise of popularity

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:32.680
around minimalism and related ideas.

00:52:32.680 --> 00:52:34.010
There is a groundswell of people

00:52:34.010 --> 00:52:37.980
who are feeling disconnected and dissatisfied in a way

00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:40.670
and are discovering this way of living

00:52:40.670 --> 00:52:43.770
that is giving their lives greater meaning and purpose.

00:52:43.770 --> 00:52:45.420
But in tandem with that,

00:52:45.420 --> 00:52:47.550
we're also seeing this acceleration

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:51.040
of our materialistic consumer culture.

00:52:51.040 --> 00:52:53.360
You see it in the film too,

00:52:53.360 --> 00:52:56.900
the kind of rapidisation of home delivery,

00:52:56.900 --> 00:52:59.780
and Amazon, and the drones and you know how everything

00:52:59.780 --> 00:53:02.110
just seems like it's on steroids right now,

00:53:02.110 --> 00:53:03.850
almost like this war of ideas

00:53:03.850 --> 00:53:07.440
that are bumping up against each other.

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:09.440
Well, you also see the sort of corporatization

00:53:09.440 --> 00:53:12.170
of simplicity as well.

00:53:12.170 --> 00:53:13.300
When Marie Kondo is selling

00:53:13.300 --> 00:53:15.126
things at the Container Store.
It could be co-opted

00:53:15.126 --> 00:53:16.480
And yeah, exactly.

00:53:16.480 --> 00:53:17.960
The container store is actually one

00:53:17.960 --> 00:53:19.150
of the biggest problems.

00:53:19.150 --> 00:53:21.750
The container store allows us to hide our hoards.

00:53:21.750 --> 00:53:23.560
And this isn't an indictment on Marie Kondo.

00:53:23.560 --> 00:53:25.870
I think she really does get to the why in her book,

00:53:25.870 --> 00:53:28.007
The Life Changing Magic of Tidying Up.

00:53:28.007 --> 00:53:30.710
She obviously discusses the how to stuff.

00:53:30.710 --> 00:53:33.430
That's her shtick, but she gets to the why.

00:53:33.430 --> 00:53:37.860
But when I see tuning forks for several hundred dollars

00:53:37.860 --> 00:53:42.860
and crystals and the commodification of simplicity,

00:53:44.100 --> 00:53:47.053
well, you don't need anything to simplify.

00:53:48.430 --> 00:53:51.280
Organizing is just well-planned hoarding.

00:53:51.280 --> 00:53:52.970
And the thing we talked about the film

00:53:52.970 --> 00:53:56.630
is the average American household has 300,000 items in it.

00:53:56.630 --> 00:53:59.850
Now, I had a really organized version of that.

00:53:59.850 --> 00:54:03.430
I had a basement full of bins and boxes

00:54:03.430 --> 00:54:07.460
and ordinal alphabetized system of CDs and DVDs.

00:54:07.460 --> 00:54:10.050
But it was just well, planned hoarding.

00:54:10.050 --> 00:54:12.120
The easiest way to organize your stuff

00:54:12.120 --> 00:54:13.760
is to get rid of anything

00:54:13.760 --> 00:54:15.270
that's no longer adding value.

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:16.160
Get rid of most of it,

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:18.640
it's so much easier to quote unquote organize,

00:54:18.640 --> 00:54:21.100
because you don't have anything that you have to organize.

00:54:21.100 --> 00:54:22.160
You're getting to the essence

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:24.820
and you're stop worrying about the form.

00:54:24.820 --> 00:54:27.669
I love that quote that you shared recently.

00:54:27.669 --> 00:54:30.967
Everything is 100% off if you don't buy it.

00:54:30.967 --> 00:54:33.310
(all chuckling)

00:54:33.310 --> 00:54:34.850
I think we shared that maybe on black Friday

00:54:34.850 --> 00:54:35.760
or something like, yeah.

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:38.823
Man I wish I could've told my 25 year old self that.

00:54:39.850 --> 00:54:42.370
He may have not been ready for it though.

00:54:42.370 --> 00:54:43.203
No.

00:54:43.203 --> 00:54:44.730
You said something real briefly

00:54:44.730 --> 00:54:48.320
about minimalism bringing meaning to people's lives.

00:54:48.320 --> 00:54:51.140
And actually, I don't know if minimalism

00:54:51.140 --> 00:54:52.650
brings meaning to people's lives.

00:54:52.650 --> 00:54:56.500
I think it's minimalism helps someone

00:54:56.500 --> 00:54:58.140
Etch-a-Sketch a new life.

00:54:58.140 --> 00:54:59.800
It helps them start over.

00:54:59.800 --> 00:55:03.430
And then from there they can start to do meaningful things.

00:55:03.430 --> 00:55:05.660
And that's really what Less Is Now is about.

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:07.460
It's about being able to start over.

00:55:09.080 --> 00:55:10.684
Thanks.

00:55:10.684 --> 00:55:12.255
However you phrased it made me think

00:55:12.255 --> 00:55:14.099
of it in a way of, I don't think minimalism is

00:55:14.099 --> 00:55:14.932
Like getting it wrong.

00:55:14.932 --> 00:55:15.765
Right.

00:55:15.765 --> 00:55:16.598
I mean, I was alluding to that,

00:55:16.598 --> 00:55:17.910
but yeah, I hear what you're saying.

00:55:17.910 --> 00:55:21.030
No, that's a very good point.

00:55:21.030 --> 00:55:22.070
The question I was gonna ask

00:55:22.070 --> 00:55:26.240
was what would you tell yourself

00:55:27.170 --> 00:55:28.370
five years ago when you were

00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:31.700
well into the minimalism thing

00:55:31.700 --> 00:55:33.550
that you understand better now

00:55:33.550 --> 00:55:36.343
that maybe you didn't then as you kind of mature?

00:55:38.250 --> 00:55:39.210
Five years ago

00:55:40.730 --> 00:55:42.423
Drop the prescriptions.

00:55:43.840 --> 00:55:46.000
Not the actual medical prescription, but

00:55:46.000 --> 00:55:48.300
You mean back to the how to thing.

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:49.440
Yeah, but also

00:55:52.940 --> 00:55:56.000
the desire to give advice to people,

00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:57.910
that's also an ego thing.

00:55:57.910 --> 00:55:58.743
I have no more advice.

00:55:58.743 --> 00:55:59.750
I have some observations,

00:55:59.750 --> 00:56:03.120
if you want to hear my own observations about my own life.

00:56:03.120 --> 00:56:05.170
But even then going back and giving advice

00:56:05.170 --> 00:56:10.170
to my 35 year old self would be almost counterintuitive.

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:14.093
Now, if I could show him some things,

00:56:15.470 --> 00:56:18.820
it'd be the peace that's related to letting go

00:56:18.820 --> 00:56:20.580
of some of the attachments,

00:56:20.580 --> 00:56:22.640
because I think Ryan and I,

00:56:22.640 --> 00:56:25.890
we picked up new attachments along the way,

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:28.640
especially when that film came out.

00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:32.250
Talk about serving your ego.

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:36.340
When you get recognized on the street a dozen times a day

00:56:36.340 --> 00:56:38.050
all of a sudden you start to believe

00:56:38.050 --> 00:56:40.250
that you're better than what you are.

00:56:40.250 --> 00:56:41.473
And Ryan and I, for a period of time,

00:56:41.473 --> 00:56:42.900
I think we went three years

00:56:42.900 --> 00:56:44.840
without doing any media as a result.

00:56:44.840 --> 00:56:49.280
I was just, it doesn't feel good to need this.

00:56:49.280 --> 00:56:51.880
And because it feels like you're creating

00:56:51.880 --> 00:56:53.280
for the wrong reason.

00:56:53.280 --> 00:56:57.440
It feels as though it's that externality

00:56:57.440 --> 00:56:58.560
you talked about.

00:56:58.560 --> 00:57:01.683
Well, that's a healthy dose of self-awareness.

00:57:03.240 --> 00:57:04.454
Maybe.

00:57:04.454 --> 00:57:09.454
I'm still working on eradicating that.

00:57:11.315 --> 00:57:12.613
Aren't we all.

00:57:12.613 --> 00:57:13.763
If you ever figure it out, let me know.

00:57:15.071 --> 00:57:15.904
But that's the thing.

00:57:15.904 --> 00:57:16.737
I don't think we ever figure it out.

00:57:16.737 --> 00:57:18.537
I think it is the eradication thing.

00:57:19.608 --> 00:57:22.310
You don't figure out heart disease,

00:57:22.310 --> 00:57:24.503
you try to eradicate it in a way.

00:57:26.060 --> 00:57:29.220
So the first movie came out in 2016.

00:57:29.220 --> 00:57:30.170
What year was that?

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:31.970
Yeah, so Netflix actually

00:57:31.970 --> 00:57:34.193
turned us down twice on that film.

00:57:35.300 --> 00:57:39.780
So we put out on our own, we did a theatrical release

00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:41.340
through a company called Gather at the time.

00:57:41.340 --> 00:57:43.190
I don't even know if they're still around

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:44.460
but it was like theatrical on demand.

00:57:44.460 --> 00:57:45.470
Was that 2016?

00:57:45.470 --> 00:57:47.880
Yeah, that was May, 2016.

00:57:47.880 --> 00:57:52.880
And we did 400 theaters, US, Canada, Australia.

00:57:53.950 --> 00:57:57.140
And then from there we went back to Netflix.

00:57:57.140 --> 00:57:58.190
They said, no, again.

00:57:59.410 --> 00:58:01.370
And so we just put it out on our own

00:58:01.370 --> 00:58:03.110
and it did relatively well.

00:58:03.110 --> 00:58:04.980
It resonated with people on iTunes,

00:58:04.980 --> 00:58:06.740
and Amazon, and other places.

00:58:06.740 --> 00:58:10.193
And so Netflix ended up saying yes to it.

00:58:10.193 --> 00:58:12.850
And that really started the conflagration

00:58:12.850 --> 00:58:15.710
because we sent the rest of our audience there

00:58:15.710 --> 00:58:17.750
who had been listening to the podcast

00:58:17.750 --> 00:58:22.660
or reading the blog, and sparked whatever algorithm does.

00:58:22.660 --> 00:58:26.197
And it showed up on a lot more people's radar.

00:58:26.197 --> 00:58:27.910
I think that's how we showed up on your radar.

00:58:27.910 --> 00:58:28.860
Yeah, I think so.

00:58:29.720 --> 00:58:31.360
Well, I think you were doing a few podcasts

00:58:31.360 --> 00:58:33.200
so I saw you guys popping up

00:58:33.200 --> 00:58:36.380
on some friends and people that I knew.

00:58:36.380 --> 00:58:38.283
And then I saw the movie.

00:58:39.140 --> 00:58:41.130
I was very struck by the movie.

00:58:41.130 --> 00:58:42.970
And then we did one of our retreats

00:58:42.970 --> 00:58:44.450
in Italy and we screened it

00:58:44.450 --> 00:58:46.960
for the group that we had there,

00:58:46.960 --> 00:58:48.092
which was really cool.

00:58:48.092 --> 00:58:48.925
That's cool.

00:58:48.925 --> 00:58:49.758
I was just remembering that today.

00:58:49.758 --> 00:58:51.330
That's awesome.

00:58:51.330 --> 00:58:53.170
And I can't remember whether I did that,

00:58:53.170 --> 00:58:55.390
I think I had interviewed you and then we went on the trip

00:58:55.390 --> 00:58:57.500
or maybe I interviewed you right afterwards.

00:58:57.500 --> 00:58:58.333
I can't remember.

00:58:58.333 --> 00:59:01.500
But I just remember being very moved by that film.

00:59:01.500 --> 00:59:04.550
And then we did the podcast and I believe

00:59:04.550 --> 00:59:07.920
that you might've even alluded either on mic or off

00:59:07.920 --> 00:59:09.670
that you guys were working on another documentary.

00:59:09.670 --> 00:59:11.021
We were.

00:59:11.021 --> 00:59:11.870
Like there was gonna be a follow-up.

00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:15.900
And here we are five, maybe almost six years later

00:59:15.900 --> 00:59:17.870
before Less Is Now premiers.

00:59:17.870 --> 00:59:19.253
So what happened?

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:23.800
We ran into some technical difficulties.

00:59:23.800 --> 00:59:25.553
Well, Ryan and I, shortly after that came out

00:59:25.553 --> 00:59:29.513
we went to Matt again and we did another tour.

00:59:30.500 --> 00:59:32.370
It was called The Less Is Now Tour.

00:59:32.370 --> 00:59:34.190
And we went out filming that,

00:59:34.190 --> 00:59:37.840
trying to sort of recreate the magic of the first film.

00:59:37.840 --> 00:59:38.750
And we put it together.

00:59:38.750 --> 00:59:42.250
We actually, we did an event at the Wilbur Theater.

00:59:42.250 --> 00:59:45.640
This where Joe Rogan shot his most recent comedy special.

00:59:45.640 --> 00:59:46.473
Beautiful spot.

00:59:46.473 --> 00:59:49.540
Yeah, we filmed it, we gave a talk there,

00:59:49.540 --> 00:59:50.690
and we were going to sort of build

00:59:50.690 --> 00:59:52.140
the film around that talk.

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:53.190
And we kind of ended up doing that

00:59:53.190 --> 00:59:55.760
in this later version of the film.

00:59:55.760 --> 00:59:58.973
And the talk looked fine

00:59:58.973 --> 01:00:02.110
but it kind of looked like a stand-up comedy special

01:00:02.110 --> 01:00:03.180
without comedy.

01:00:03.180 --> 01:00:04.680
You were like waiting for the punch,

01:00:04.680 --> 01:00:05.930
because it was the venue.

01:00:07.253 --> 01:00:08.810
And so it just, it fell flat.

01:00:08.810 --> 01:00:10.212
It didn't work.

01:00:10.212 --> 01:00:11.045
And so we did it again.

01:00:11.045 --> 01:00:11.878
We actually brought you out,

01:00:11.878 --> 01:00:14.200
we rented out this giant warehouse space.

01:00:14.200 --> 01:00:16.808
That was like two years ago.

01:00:16.808 --> 01:00:17.816
Almost three, yeah.

01:00:17.816 --> 01:00:18.649
Matt was there, there was tons of,

01:00:18.649 --> 01:00:19.633
was it that long ago?
Almost three

01:00:19.633 --> 01:00:21.350
Almost three, yeah.

01:00:21.350 --> 01:00:22.797
And this was the big shoot.
Yeah, it was 2018

01:00:22.797 --> 01:00:23.760
And you had a live audience.

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:26.940
I mean, that warehouse space is in the movie.

01:00:26.940 --> 01:00:28.547
Was that from that evening

01:00:28.547 --> 01:00:29.380
No.

01:00:29.380 --> 01:00:30.322
or did you guys end up re-shooting that?

01:00:30.322 --> 01:00:31.662
That was from a year and a half later.

01:00:31.662 --> 01:00:33.717
A year and a half later we went to the same studio.

01:00:33.717 --> 01:00:34.550
Wow.

01:00:34.550 --> 01:00:35.383
'Cause I was telling Julie,

01:00:35.383 --> 01:00:36.216
we were watching it, and I was like,

01:00:36.216 --> 01:00:37.049
"Yeah, I was there that night

01:00:37.049 --> 01:00:37.882
"when these guys were doing this.

01:00:37.882 --> 01:00:40.827
"but, I'm like, "it doesn't look like there's people there.

01:00:40.827 --> 01:00:43.597
"Like when I was there, there was a lot of people there.

01:00:43.597 --> 01:00:45.070
"So something was weird."

01:00:45.070 --> 01:00:47.010
Yeah, something was different about it.

01:00:47.010 --> 01:00:49.110
Yeah, so it went from a comedy special,

01:00:49.110 --> 01:00:52.653
to no comedy, to a really well done TEDx Talk.

01:00:54.070 --> 01:00:55.800
That's what it looked like.

01:00:55.800 --> 01:00:57.960
So that night you were there, you actually came up

01:00:57.960 --> 01:01:00.130
you're gracious enough to do the intro for us.

01:01:00.130 --> 01:01:03.000
And we gave a talk in front of two different crowds

01:01:03.000 --> 01:01:03.833
because we figured,

01:01:03.833 --> 01:01:06.660
well the Wilbur Theater aesthetic was the problem.

01:01:06.660 --> 01:01:10.730
And so we'll refilm this in front of a beautiful aesthetic,

01:01:10.730 --> 01:01:11.910
this old abandoned warehouse,

01:01:11.910 --> 01:01:15.080
we'll build the set for it, bring a crew out, film it.

01:01:15.080 --> 01:01:17.440
And it'll work out that way.

01:01:17.440 --> 01:01:18.400
That's the problem.

01:01:18.400 --> 01:01:20.484
Well then, so we get there

01:01:20.484 --> 01:01:21.695
and then we start interspersing it

01:01:21.695 --> 01:01:23.240
with these documentary elements.

01:01:23.240 --> 01:01:27.023
And it was like mixing vegan cheesecake,

01:01:28.320 --> 01:01:33.320
shout out to Rich Roll, and sweet and sour tofu

01:01:34.490 --> 01:01:36.460
and just mushing them two together.

01:01:36.460 --> 01:01:39.180
And you're like, I really like both of these things

01:01:40.390 --> 01:01:42.930
but they don't work when I smash them together.

01:01:42.930 --> 01:01:44.530
And so we had to go back to the drawing board

01:01:44.530 --> 01:01:47.790
and we said, hey, we're just going to scrap.

01:01:47.790 --> 01:01:49.690
So that was the second time we did the film.

01:01:49.690 --> 01:01:52.510
We scrapped the whole thing and started over again.

01:01:52.510 --> 01:01:53.780
And so this project we thought

01:01:53.780 --> 01:01:55.500
was gonna take us about four months

01:01:55.500 --> 01:02:00.500
ended up taking about four years from the inception until.

01:02:00.680 --> 01:02:02.580
'Cause when I was there that night

01:02:02.580 --> 01:02:04.330
my sense was that you guys were

01:02:04.330 --> 01:02:06.560
in the final throes of wrapping this thing up.

01:02:06.560 --> 01:02:09.440
Oh, we were, of that version.

01:02:09.440 --> 01:02:11.364
And those two will never see the light of day.

01:02:11.364 --> 01:02:12.700
It's not that they were terrible.

01:02:12.700 --> 01:02:14.250
They just didn't work

01:02:14.250 --> 01:02:16.570
for what we were trying to communicate.

01:02:16.570 --> 01:02:17.403
We knew

01:02:19.900 --> 01:02:22.300
we weren't really putting our best foot forward.

01:02:22.300 --> 01:02:24.020
As Ryan said, it was like

01:02:24.020 --> 01:02:26.170
it would've been a really good YouTube video.

01:02:26.170 --> 01:02:27.620
Yeah, it would've been a great YouTube video.

01:02:27.620 --> 01:02:29.580
I was just gonna say, just to add onto that,

01:02:29.580 --> 01:02:30.753
when we got those two films back,

01:02:30.753 --> 01:02:32.940
I just remember not getting the feeling

01:02:32.940 --> 01:02:35.190
that I got with Minimalism.

01:02:35.190 --> 01:02:38.090
For what we gave Matt, he did an amazing job,

01:02:38.090 --> 01:02:39.233
for what we gave him.

01:02:40.070 --> 01:02:42.510
But yeah, it's been a long road.

01:02:42.510 --> 01:02:43.510
Well, it's tough too,

01:02:43.510 --> 01:02:46.780
because the first one had a built-in narrative

01:02:46.780 --> 01:02:48.370
and you guys are these underdogs

01:02:48.370 --> 01:02:50.080
and you're going on this trip.

01:02:50.080 --> 01:02:54.820
So there's kind of a tempo or a propulsion to that.

01:02:54.820 --> 01:02:56.550
And in the follow-up,

01:02:56.550 --> 01:02:58.415
then the challenge is like, well,

01:02:58.415 --> 01:03:00.617
what's the next chapter of this

01:03:00.617 --> 01:03:02.939
and how do we make that compelling?

01:03:02.939 --> 01:03:04.293
I think that chapter ended up being the chapter before

01:03:04.293 --> 01:03:06.534
like it was in a weird way,

01:03:06.534 --> 01:03:08.650
it was the first ever documentary prequel.

01:03:08.650 --> 01:03:10.823
It doesn't require you to see the first film, obviously.

01:03:10.823 --> 01:03:12.570
Yeah, not at all.

01:03:12.570 --> 01:03:14.530
And they're two independent things.

01:03:14.530 --> 01:03:16.330
And this one, we were really aggressive

01:03:16.330 --> 01:03:17.720
about keeping it under an hour

01:03:17.720 --> 01:03:20.870
in the whole spirit of minimalism.

01:03:20.870 --> 01:03:25.240
But in doing that, we had to cut out a lot of amazing.

01:03:25.240 --> 01:03:28.300
Ryan had this whole sub narrative about this.

01:03:28.300 --> 01:03:29.350
We can talk about it here.

01:03:29.350 --> 01:03:30.880
'Cause you haven't really talked about it publicly.

01:03:30.880 --> 01:03:31.760
The whole drug thing.

01:03:31.760 --> 01:03:34.183
I was like you, Rich, I loved to party.

01:03:35.090 --> 01:03:37.890
This is something that I know about you

01:03:37.890 --> 01:03:41.450
but I've never heard you actually talk about.

01:03:41.450 --> 01:03:43.470
You've had your run in with drugs and alcohol.

01:03:43.470 --> 01:03:44.729
For sure.

01:03:44.729 --> 01:03:45.562
I talk about it whenever it comes up

01:03:45.562 --> 01:03:48.000
like I'm totally okay to talk, especially coming from Ohio.

01:03:48.000 --> 01:03:50.110
'Cause like I hope someone in Ohio

01:03:50.110 --> 01:03:52.800
who is hooked on pain pills right now

01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:54.380
is listening to this and knowing

01:03:54.380 --> 01:03:57.370
that they can totally pull themselves out of that situation.

01:03:57.370 --> 01:03:59.730
'Cause I mean, Dayton, Ohio, where we're from,

01:03:59.730 --> 01:04:01.230
I don't know if it still is,

01:04:01.230 --> 01:04:04.360
but it was the overdose capital of the world.

01:04:04.360 --> 01:04:08.960
So there's just a lot of drugs there.

01:04:08.960 --> 01:04:10.500
But yeah, to Josh's point,

01:04:10.500 --> 01:04:14.650
there was this beautiful arc in the film

01:04:14.650 --> 01:04:16.650
that we couldn't put in there

01:04:16.650 --> 01:04:17.707
that I really tried to get.

01:04:17.707 --> 01:04:19.730
And Matt tried to put it, it just didn't work.

01:04:19.730 --> 01:04:22.760
But it was about kind of going down that road

01:04:22.760 --> 01:04:25.140
and what pain that caused me

01:04:25.140 --> 01:04:28.000
and how I was able to kind of start over.

01:04:28.000 --> 01:04:30.760
But it's really, it's those stories,

01:04:30.760 --> 01:04:32.210
again, unfortunately it couldn't make the film,

01:04:32.210 --> 01:04:33.420
but I think it's those stories

01:04:33.420 --> 01:04:35.610
that really mean the most to people.

01:04:35.610 --> 01:04:37.410
They want to hear about,

01:04:37.410 --> 01:04:40.130
oh, Ryan isn't this perfect person

01:04:40.130 --> 01:04:43.310
who is just one day like, oh I'm gonna simplify my life.

01:04:43.310 --> 01:04:44.670
And I mean, it was a lot of work,

01:04:44.670 --> 01:04:45.503
and it was a lot of pain,

01:04:45.503 --> 01:04:46.660
and it was a lot of discomfort,

01:04:46.660 --> 01:04:49.350
And I did get through it,

01:04:49.350 --> 01:04:53.100
but yeah, I was living in the, is it an opiate?

01:04:53.100 --> 01:04:55.210
Is that what pain pills are?

01:04:55.210 --> 01:04:56.330
They're opiates?

01:04:56.330 --> 01:04:57.397
Many of them are.

01:04:58.560 --> 01:05:01.030
So yeah, opiates were real easy access.

01:05:01.030 --> 01:05:04.680
And when I was working 60, 70,

01:05:04.680 --> 01:05:09.120
sometimes 80 hours a week, I would caffeinate myself.

01:05:09.120 --> 01:05:10.470
I didn't do a lot of coke,

01:05:10.470 --> 01:05:12.760
mainly because my mom like turned me off

01:05:12.760 --> 01:05:15.787
to that drug because I saw her go through her own thing.

01:05:15.787 --> 01:05:18.137
And I talk about that in the first documentary.

01:05:19.270 --> 01:05:22.740
But I would caffeinate myself, Adderall,

01:05:22.740 --> 01:05:25.190
whatever productivity thing I could get in my body.

01:05:25.190 --> 01:05:28.050
But then you get home at nine o'clock at night

01:05:28.050 --> 01:05:29.820
and it's hard to unwind,

01:05:29.820 --> 01:05:33.430
but it's really easy with a pain pill and a beer and yeah.

01:05:33.430 --> 01:05:34.878
So I got kinda trapped in that.

01:05:34.878 --> 01:05:36.380
So it wasn't going out to the bars and getting loaded.

01:05:36.380 --> 01:05:37.664
It was more.

01:05:37.664 --> 01:05:39.160
It was some of that too.

01:05:39.160 --> 01:05:41.450
Right, okay, I feel better.

01:05:41.450 --> 01:05:43.280
Unfortunately, we even did recreation scenes

01:05:43.280 --> 01:05:45.750
of Ryan getting beat up at a bar once,

01:05:45.750 --> 01:05:47.270
that didn't make the film.

01:05:47.270 --> 01:05:48.900
We're actually gonna find a way to put a lot

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:51.190
of this on Patreon or somewhere.

01:05:51.190 --> 01:05:52.560
I actually just got permission

01:05:52.560 --> 01:05:57.200
from Netflix today to use some of these scenes somewhere.

01:05:57.200 --> 01:05:59.870
And Ryan talked about it in the film

01:05:59.870 --> 01:06:02.513
but like we're spending upwards of $5,000 a month

01:06:02.513 --> 01:06:04.240
I remember telling him

01:06:04.240 --> 01:06:05.572
On opioids,

01:06:05.572 --> 01:06:06.405
I'm like, I might have a problem.

01:06:07.612 --> 01:06:08.445
I was talking to my therapist about it,

01:06:08.445 --> 01:06:10.680
and I remember mentioning that to them.

01:06:10.680 --> 01:06:12.900
And she was like, "Eh",

01:06:12.900 --> 01:06:14.127
She's like, "I've seen worse.

01:06:14.127 --> 01:06:16.657
"I've seen people spending $10 to $15,000

01:06:16.657 --> 01:06:17.680
"a month on drugs."

01:06:17.680 --> 01:06:19.320
Time for a new therapist.

01:06:19.320 --> 01:06:20.493
Yeah, right.

01:06:20.493 --> 01:06:21.914
She's like, "You're not that bad."

01:06:21.914 --> 01:06:23.580
I think he was just trying to make me not feel so bad.

01:06:23.580 --> 01:06:26.320
So did you just go cold turkey

01:06:26.320 --> 01:06:27.416
Oh no.

01:06:27.416 --> 01:06:28.249
and put it in the rear view

01:06:28.249 --> 01:06:29.967
or how did you get past it?

01:06:29.967 --> 01:06:30.800
I went through it.

01:06:30.800 --> 01:06:33.330
I probably should have done a 12 step program,

01:06:33.330 --> 01:06:38.330
but I went off of opiates to get on,

01:06:38.690 --> 01:06:40.330
there's a product called like

01:06:40.330 --> 01:06:41.163
Suboxone?

01:06:41.163 --> 01:06:42.430
Suboxone, yeah.

01:06:42.430 --> 01:06:46.630
And you don't really, it's not like methadone.

01:06:46.630 --> 01:06:48.370
Methadone is something that you just go

01:06:48.370 --> 01:06:52.050
from illegal addictions to legal addictions,

01:06:52.050 --> 01:06:53.980
where Suboxone works a little bit different in the brain.

01:06:53.980 --> 01:06:56.160
So I was on that program for a little bit,

01:06:56.160 --> 01:06:57.530
getting a lot of therapy.

01:06:57.530 --> 01:07:02.410
And I remember one time I was going on a trip

01:07:02.410 --> 01:07:05.070
and I had forgot to bring my Suboxone

01:07:05.070 --> 01:07:07.680
and I went like two weeks without it.

01:07:07.680 --> 01:07:10.050
And I was like, oh, I'm free.

01:07:10.050 --> 01:07:10.883
I'm done.

01:07:10.883 --> 01:07:12.260
I don't have to do this anymore.

01:07:12.260 --> 01:07:13.889
And you just never went back.

01:07:13.889 --> 01:07:14.722
Never went back, yeah.

01:07:14.722 --> 01:07:15.555
Wow.

01:07:15.555 --> 01:07:16.480
Yeah, man.

01:07:16.480 --> 01:07:17.313
Good for you, man.

01:07:17.313 --> 01:07:18.530
Yeah, it was a long road.

01:07:18.530 --> 01:07:22.380
I wouldn't wish that experience on my worst enemy.

01:07:22.380 --> 01:07:24.930
The detox from opioids is horrible.

01:07:24.930 --> 01:07:26.030
It's miserable.

01:07:26.030 --> 01:07:28.810
I still don't think I've recovered 100%.

01:07:28.810 --> 01:07:31.390
Like mentally, you mean, like brain function wise?

01:07:31.390 --> 01:07:33.060
Sleep wise, I think.

01:07:33.060 --> 01:07:35.240
Yeah your deep sleep's still way off, right?

01:07:35.240 --> 01:07:36.110
Yeah, I'm lucky to get like

01:07:36.110 --> 01:07:38.900
You guys are both rocking the Oura ring, I can tell.

01:07:38.900 --> 01:07:39.762
Yeah.

01:07:39.762 --> 01:07:42.670
So yeah, it was a long road,

01:07:42.670 --> 01:07:47.200
but when I started living intentionally

01:07:47.200 --> 01:07:49.233
and really facing these things head on,

01:07:51.553 --> 01:07:52.386
that wasn't the magic answer

01:07:52.386 --> 01:07:54.223
but it certainly gave me some leverage.

01:07:55.160 --> 01:07:56.820
Well, alluding to something

01:07:56.820 --> 01:07:58.750
that I asked you about earlier.

01:07:58.750 --> 01:08:00.210
I mean, both of you guys survived

01:08:00.210 --> 01:08:02.273
quite a bit of childhood trauma.

01:08:04.430 --> 01:08:05.550
Are you in therapy?

01:08:05.550 --> 01:08:07.460
How have you worked through?

01:08:07.460 --> 01:08:09.650
You seem to have a pretty healthy

01:08:09.650 --> 01:08:12.670
relationship with your past now.

01:08:12.670 --> 01:08:14.050
It doesn't feel like you're holding onto

01:08:14.050 --> 01:08:15.080
a lot of resentment

01:08:16.919 --> 01:08:17.752
No.

01:08:17.752 --> 01:08:18.585
and those kinds of negative patterns.

01:08:18.585 --> 01:08:20.603
So how did you work through that?

01:08:20.603 --> 01:08:23.260
I think trauma's perspectival in many ways.

01:08:23.260 --> 01:08:27.270
Like I talk about this in the film.

01:08:27.270 --> 01:08:30.400
So my very first memory as a child

01:08:30.400 --> 01:08:33.570
is my father extinguishing a cigarette on Mom's chest.

01:08:33.570 --> 01:08:35.442
Now of course, I'm going to remember something like that.

01:08:35.442 --> 01:08:36.410
That's horrible.

01:08:36.410 --> 01:08:38.440
But I also don't think it traumatized me

01:08:38.440 --> 01:08:40.140
the same way that other things trauma.

01:08:40.140 --> 01:08:40.973
And so, it's weird.

01:08:40.973 --> 01:08:42.500
Like I do remember that

01:08:42.500 --> 01:08:45.324
and I'm sure it was awful or whatever

01:08:45.324 --> 01:08:49.920
but there are other things that seem less consequential

01:08:49.920 --> 01:08:51.400
but traumatized me even more.

01:08:51.400 --> 01:08:52.340
I was much more worried

01:08:52.340 --> 01:08:54.760
of Child Protective Services taking me away

01:08:54.760 --> 01:08:56.630
because my mom was an alcoholic.

01:08:56.630 --> 01:08:59.713
I think I was far more traumatized by that.

01:09:03.220 --> 01:09:06.764
I certainly have a lot of forms of sort of OCD.

01:09:06.764 --> 01:09:09.130
I've been diagnosed with OCD, but it's like low level.

01:09:09.130 --> 01:09:10.860
I'm not like painting my floor every day

01:09:10.860 --> 01:09:11.790
or anything like that.

01:09:11.790 --> 01:09:16.790
But I'm sure that's a whole thing about control,

01:09:17.410 --> 01:09:20.570
because of the chaos of childhood.

01:09:20.570 --> 01:09:22.320
It's almost like you have this bar of like,

01:09:22.320 --> 01:09:24.400
well, I'm not painting my floor.

01:09:24.400 --> 01:09:25.233
Yeah.

01:09:27.591 --> 01:09:28.630
But just like anything

01:09:28.630 --> 01:09:31.130
you can have an unhealthy relationship to a good thing.

01:09:31.130 --> 01:09:33.070
I'm sure that you could have an unhealthy relationship

01:09:33.070 --> 01:09:34.620
to minimalism where it is,

01:09:34.620 --> 01:09:38.710
I am absolutely in control of my environment all the time.

01:09:38.710 --> 01:09:40.950
The solution becomes the problem.

01:09:40.950 --> 01:09:44.020
And that's why the whole anti prescription thing

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:45.260
for me now.

01:09:45.260 --> 01:09:46.370
Yeah, I've been doing a lot more

01:09:46.370 --> 01:09:49.420
exploration this year around around that.

01:09:49.420 --> 01:09:50.253
But

01:09:53.380 --> 01:09:54.790
we don't actually need the solutions.

01:09:54.790 --> 01:09:56.440
If your chair's on fire right now, Rich,

01:09:56.440 --> 01:09:57.273
you're not gonna say, hey,

01:09:57.273 --> 01:09:59.070
can you hand me that fire safety manual over there?

01:09:59.070 --> 01:10:01.550
Because it would really help me out right now.

01:10:01.550 --> 01:10:05.360
You don't need three steps to get out of a flaming chair.

01:10:05.360 --> 01:10:06.340
You're going to do it

01:10:06.340 --> 01:10:07.200
and you're gonna do it like that

01:10:07.200 --> 01:10:09.170
because your why is so powerful,

01:10:09.170 --> 01:10:11.740
the how takes care of itself.

01:10:11.740 --> 01:10:16.170
And so the problem is not a lack of instructions.

01:10:16.170 --> 01:10:19.510
The problem is that your butt is on fire.

01:10:19.510 --> 01:10:24.510
And so I think that too often we,

01:10:26.700 --> 01:10:28.010
any answer I give you here

01:10:28.010 --> 01:10:29.620
will sort of be like a narrative overlay

01:10:29.620 --> 01:10:31.540
with respect to the trauma or whatever.

01:10:31.540 --> 01:10:34.813
But I think it does have to do with detachment ultimately.

01:10:34.813 --> 01:10:37.220
And maybe there's a healthy detachment

01:10:37.220 --> 01:10:38.470
and unhealthy detachment.

01:10:38.470 --> 01:10:43.363
I don't know, but a healthy detachment from,

01:10:45.644 --> 01:10:48.680
I don't cling to that anymore.

01:10:48.680 --> 01:10:49.970
I'm so glad you asked this question

01:10:49.970 --> 01:10:51.800
about our childhoods,

01:10:51.800 --> 01:10:54.510
because I did hold on to a lot of resentment

01:10:54.510 --> 01:10:56.093
for my mother and father.

01:10:56.960 --> 01:11:01.560
And it was mainly because I felt kind of gypped

01:11:01.560 --> 01:11:05.100
of like, why would they do those things?

01:11:05.100 --> 01:11:06.300
Why didn't they?

01:11:06.300 --> 01:11:08.190
They're the parents, I'm the child.

01:11:08.190 --> 01:11:10.630
They should be able to know what to do.

01:11:10.630 --> 01:11:14.520
And any normal parent would put their child first.

01:11:14.520 --> 01:11:17.550
And I had all these narratives

01:11:17.550 --> 01:11:19.770
and all these questions.

01:11:19.770 --> 01:11:20.823
And through therapy,

01:11:22.040 --> 01:11:24.950
and I got to this recently within the last few years,

01:11:24.950 --> 01:11:26.700
I mean, I've always tried to have

01:11:26.700 --> 01:11:29.640
a good relationship with my parents with that resentment,

01:11:29.640 --> 01:11:34.193
but I was able to let a lot of it go when,

01:11:34.193 --> 01:11:35.026
it was a couple of years ago,

01:11:35.026 --> 01:11:36.580
seeing a therapist here in LA,

01:11:36.580 --> 01:11:39.800
and he just kind of helped me get to this point

01:11:39.800 --> 01:11:44.800
of when things really started to hit the fan,

01:11:46.090 --> 01:11:50.253
my parents were probably 30, maybe 32,

01:11:51.287 --> 01:11:53.293
they're younger than I am now.

01:11:54.710 --> 01:11:56.740
That's a trip when you realize that.

01:11:56.740 --> 01:11:58.910
Yeah, it is, it was.

01:11:58.910 --> 01:12:00.510
And I was like, wait a minute,

01:12:00.510 --> 01:12:02.490
here I am with this narrative of like

01:12:02.490 --> 01:12:04.420
they should have known what to do.

01:12:04.420 --> 01:12:05.820
And I'm like, I'm 37 years old.

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:08.190
I have no idea what the hell I'm doing.

01:12:08.190 --> 01:12:12.653
And for me to put that pressure on them wasn't fair.

01:12:14.730 --> 01:12:15.610
I didn't see it as fair.

01:12:15.610 --> 01:12:17.510
And I finally looked at them.

01:12:17.510 --> 01:12:18.343
I'm like, oh.

01:12:19.347 --> 01:12:21.248
'Cause you know, I'd love to sit here, Rich,

01:12:21.248 --> 01:12:22.507
and be like, oh, I've got a perfect life.

01:12:22.507 --> 01:12:23.350
Become a minimalist and you're gonna live a perfect life.

01:12:23.350 --> 01:12:25.750
But I still struggle with a lot of things.

01:12:25.750 --> 01:12:28.350
And I've got my own battles that I'm fighting.

01:12:28.350 --> 01:12:31.510
And my parents, I finally was able to see,

01:12:31.510 --> 01:12:34.350
like, oh, they've got their own shit going on.

01:12:34.350 --> 01:12:36.490
They've got their own battles that they're fighting.

01:12:36.490 --> 01:12:38.230
And they're still fighting them to this day.

01:12:38.230 --> 01:12:39.930
And I'm really glad I don't have those battles.

01:12:39.930 --> 01:12:44.650
However, understanding that I'm 39 years old.

01:12:44.650 --> 01:12:46.700
I'm for all intents and purposes,

01:12:46.700 --> 01:12:50.110
just as confused as they were at 32 years old.

01:12:50.110 --> 01:12:53.580
And seeing that in them really helped me

01:12:53.580 --> 01:12:56.940
drop those poor me, why me,

01:12:56.940 --> 01:12:58.530
why didn't they, they should have,

01:12:58.530 --> 01:12:59.920
it really helped me drop a lot of that.

01:12:59.920 --> 01:13:00.870
Right, right, right.

01:13:00.870 --> 01:13:03.760
And that those behaviors aren't necessarily

01:13:03.760 --> 01:13:07.490
a referendum on how much they loved you or didn't love you.

01:13:07.490 --> 01:13:08.323
Absolutely.

01:13:08.323 --> 01:13:10.303
But just their own personal limiters, it?

01:13:10.303 --> 01:13:11.873
God, it felt like it at the time.

01:13:11.873 --> 01:13:12.706
When I was 30, I was a disaster.

01:13:12.706 --> 01:13:14.320
You and me both.

01:13:14.320 --> 01:13:17.502
It definitely felt like it at the time though,

01:13:17.502 --> 01:13:21.220
growing up, like, if you loved me, you'd stop drinking

01:13:21.220 --> 01:13:22.053
sort of thing.

01:13:25.254 --> 01:13:26.087
And yeah.

01:13:27.370 --> 01:13:29.950
Ryan and I grew up in similar sort of situations.

01:13:29.950 --> 01:13:32.730
I would argue his situation was far worse than mine

01:13:32.730 --> 01:13:33.889
in many respects.

01:13:33.889 --> 01:13:34.960
No way.

01:13:34.960 --> 01:13:36.210
Let's battle right now on.

01:13:36.210 --> 01:13:38.750
The SWAT Team never raided my home.

01:13:38.750 --> 01:13:39.900
Oh yeah, that's true.

01:13:40.970 --> 01:13:42.657
All right, you got me.
That's heavy, man,

01:13:42.657 --> 01:13:43.740
that's heavy.

01:13:44.620 --> 01:13:49.620
But what I'll say is that Ryan saw the partying

01:13:51.290 --> 01:13:53.270
growing up as a fun thing.

01:13:53.270 --> 01:13:55.500
He saw his Mom in particular

01:13:55.500 --> 01:13:58.430
having fun with drugs and alcohol.

01:13:58.430 --> 01:14:01.210
I saw my mother being depressed with drugs and alcohol.

01:14:01.210 --> 01:14:03.160
I've never had a drink in my life

01:14:03.160 --> 01:14:08.160
because I saw the sort of terminus of this thing.

01:14:08.440 --> 01:14:11.840
Where Ryan saw what he thought was the terminus,

01:14:11.840 --> 01:14:14.380
the excitement, the joy, the pleasure.

01:14:14.380 --> 01:14:15.490
It wasn't real joy.

01:14:15.490 --> 01:14:17.260
It was confusing joy with pleasure.

01:14:17.260 --> 01:14:19.923
Yeah, Mom's house was like the party house.

01:14:19.923 --> 01:14:21.406
I remember people would just pop in random.

01:14:21.406 --> 01:14:23.120
We had a pool and like fenced in backyard.

01:14:23.120 --> 01:14:24.803
But it was an above ground pool.

01:14:27.506 --> 01:14:28.710
Yeah, but good times.

01:14:28.710 --> 01:14:29.543
Right, exactly.

01:14:29.543 --> 01:14:30.780
But that's all you needed

01:14:30.780 --> 01:14:33.150
and a bunch of drugs and alcohol

01:14:33.150 --> 01:14:36.087
and everyone's having a good time.

01:14:36.087 --> 01:14:37.320
So it's fascinating to me that you guys

01:14:37.320 --> 01:14:39.853
have best friends since fifth grade.

01:14:41.036 --> 01:14:45.360
You're now in 10 years of doing this thing together,

01:14:45.360 --> 01:14:47.073
so you're in business together.

01:14:49.090 --> 01:14:52.900
I've never seen you guys argue or act short with each other.

01:14:52.900 --> 01:14:54.070
You must have your moments.

01:14:54.070 --> 01:14:55.140
I mean, come on.

01:14:55.140 --> 01:14:57.390
But I mean, how have you,

01:14:57.390 --> 01:14:59.930
how do you take care of this relationship?

01:14:59.930 --> 01:15:01.881
The Minimalists exposed.

01:15:01.881 --> 01:15:03.979
How do you make sure that you guys

01:15:03.979 --> 01:15:06.920
are good with each other?

01:15:06.920 --> 01:15:08.830
It's gotta be challenging at times.

01:15:08.830 --> 01:15:11.880
I think Josh is the most tolerant person I know.

01:15:11.880 --> 01:15:14.500
So he's just able to put up with all my crap.

01:15:14.500 --> 01:15:15.825
That's what it is.

01:15:15.825 --> 01:15:17.360
That's what it is, it has nothing to do with me.

01:15:17.360 --> 01:15:18.930
It's a joke.

01:15:18.930 --> 01:15:21.340
We have an acronym that we often talk about.

01:15:21.340 --> 01:15:25.777
Shout out to Patrick Roan who had the conversation with us

01:15:27.130 --> 01:15:28.070
that spurred this acronym.

01:15:28.070 --> 01:15:28.903
It's Tara, TARA.

01:15:30.191 --> 01:15:34.170
But if I really want to understand someone,

01:15:35.410 --> 01:15:36.543
truly understand them,

01:15:38.640 --> 01:15:41.220
that's the sort of the process that I go through

01:15:41.220 --> 01:15:42.990
of tolerating the person first.

01:15:42.990 --> 01:15:45.080
It's a weak virtue and it's not

01:15:45.080 --> 01:15:46.200
gonna get you very far.

01:15:46.200 --> 01:15:47.650
Tolerance.

01:15:47.650 --> 01:15:49.440
There's a, what is it on the I-10

01:15:49.440 --> 01:15:50.560
whenever we're driving an Ella

01:15:50.560 --> 01:15:52.790
is acting crazy in the car.

01:15:52.790 --> 01:15:54.910
There's a sign that says museum of tolerance.

01:15:54.910 --> 01:15:56.480
And I always joke with Ella,

01:15:56.480 --> 01:15:57.313
The Robertson.

01:15:57.313 --> 01:15:59.140
that our car is the museum of tolerance

01:15:59.140 --> 01:16:00.653
whenever she's in the car.

01:16:02.970 --> 01:16:05.500
The mobile museum of tolerance anyway.

01:16:05.500 --> 01:16:06.560
Anyway, tolerate.

01:16:06.560 --> 01:16:08.270
And then we go from there,

01:16:08.270 --> 01:16:09.790
we take these, these steps.

01:16:09.790 --> 01:16:11.523
Tolerance is a good first step.

01:16:13.330 --> 01:16:15.923
And then we move on to acceptance,

01:16:17.600 --> 01:16:19.890
respect, and then ultimately appreciation.

01:16:19.890 --> 01:16:23.340
And Ryan and I have radically different beliefs.

01:16:23.340 --> 01:16:24.680
I'm trying to let go of those beliefs.

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:26.420
I don't think beliefs serve us very well

01:16:26.420 --> 01:16:28.433
but that's a different conversation.

01:16:29.400 --> 01:16:31.400
We have the same values though.

01:16:31.400 --> 01:16:34.550
And because we have the same values,

01:16:34.550 --> 01:16:35.900
when we have different beliefs,

01:16:35.900 --> 01:16:39.035
so we get there via different paths, so to speak.

01:16:39.035 --> 01:16:40.470
We have different opinions.

01:16:40.470 --> 01:16:41.790
We have different personalities.

01:16:41.790 --> 01:16:44.490
We certainly have different preferences.

01:16:44.490 --> 01:16:45.720
But I don't say, well,

01:16:45.720 --> 01:16:47.143
his preferences are right or wrong.

01:16:47.143 --> 01:16:49.380
I might think it at first,

01:16:49.380 --> 01:16:53.440
but that's also my ego talking

01:16:53.440 --> 01:16:56.060
and I don't want to just tolerate his preferences.

01:16:56.060 --> 01:16:58.050
That's not gonna make for a good friendship,

01:16:58.050 --> 01:16:59.661
a good business relationship.

01:16:59.661 --> 01:17:01.340
And by the way, it doesn't ever feel like a business.

01:17:01.340 --> 01:17:02.580
I don't think we had business paperwork

01:17:02.580 --> 01:17:05.003
until about four years into the thing.

01:17:06.680 --> 01:17:08.615
Even now we don't have,

01:17:08.615 --> 01:17:12.690
it's not something we think about that way.

01:17:12.690 --> 01:17:14.880
Yeah, it's a business, we employ some people,

01:17:14.880 --> 01:17:19.680
but we go beyond tolerance.

01:17:19.680 --> 01:17:23.130
We accept the fact that we're both gonna be different.

01:17:23.130 --> 01:17:24.700
I'm not trying to change you.

01:17:24.700 --> 01:17:28.991
It's not about, here's what you must do.

01:17:28.991 --> 01:17:31.170
And then of course the respect thing

01:17:31.170 --> 01:17:32.900
of course I respect your preferences.

01:17:32.900 --> 01:17:36.293
I don't always completely understand them, but that's okay.

01:17:37.360 --> 01:17:40.060
And ultimately, if you can get to that level

01:17:40.060 --> 01:17:44.060
where you actually appreciate the idiosyncrasies

01:17:44.060 --> 01:17:48.400
then that's like full sort of detachment,

01:17:48.400 --> 01:17:50.110
not needing to change someone

01:17:50.110 --> 01:17:53.513
I think, and this goes for any relationship.

01:17:55.510 --> 01:17:57.880
First off, if Josh comes to me with something,

01:17:57.880 --> 01:17:59.890
it's not accusatory,

01:17:59.890 --> 01:18:02.243
it's like something he wants to talk about.

01:18:03.330 --> 01:18:07.290
And when you approach a situation like that,

01:18:07.290 --> 01:18:11.770
you can interpret it a lot differently

01:18:11.770 --> 01:18:14.393
than like, oh Josh is trying to shame me.

01:18:14.393 --> 01:18:16.153
I never feel like he's trying to shame me.

01:18:16.153 --> 01:18:17.640
And I think that is where arguments come in.

01:18:17.640 --> 01:18:19.760
When people start to try and shame other people

01:18:19.760 --> 01:18:21.510
and that's, oh you just shamed me,

01:18:21.510 --> 01:18:23.240
now I'm gonna shame you.

01:18:23.240 --> 01:18:26.110
And now we're gonna trade shames back and forth.

01:18:26.110 --> 01:18:30.950
Where I try to be the Zen Buddhist

01:18:30.950 --> 01:18:33.760
that Josh is when it comes to confrontation,

01:18:33.760 --> 01:18:35.230
but sometimes I'll go to Josh with something

01:18:35.230 --> 01:18:36.629
and he'll be like,

01:18:36.629 --> 01:18:37.747
"I know you don't mean to say it that way.

01:18:37.747 --> 01:18:40.360
"I think what you're trying to say is this.

01:18:40.360 --> 01:18:42.620
And I'm like, "Yeah, you're absolutely right."

01:18:42.620 --> 01:18:46.287
And so when I say it's Josh, it truly is,

01:18:46.287 --> 01:18:49.860
when he comes to me, it's absolutely non-confrontational.

01:18:49.860 --> 01:18:50.693
And if I go to him

01:18:50.693 --> 01:18:52.330
and I'm accidentally being confrontational

01:18:52.330 --> 01:18:54.170
he's able to process it and be like,

01:18:54.170 --> 01:18:57.150
I know Ryan's not being a jerk right now.

01:18:57.150 --> 01:18:59.557
So yeah, let's get to the root of

01:18:59.557 --> 01:19:00.759
That's some ninja shit though.

01:19:00.759 --> 01:19:02.910
It's hard to master that

01:19:02.910 --> 01:19:05.453
and have that kind of presence of mind.

01:19:06.650 --> 01:19:07.483
It's really inspiring.

01:19:07.483 --> 01:19:09.060
Like for me and my wife,

01:19:09.060 --> 01:19:10.990
I really go out of my way

01:19:10.990 --> 01:19:13.040
to try and have that approach with her.

01:19:13.040 --> 01:19:17.110
Again, I'm not perfect, but I do better.

01:19:17.110 --> 01:19:19.140
Every day I get a little better, I think.

01:19:19.140 --> 01:19:20.690
I used to be competitive

01:19:22.240 --> 01:19:25.253
but that's like saying I used to be mentally ill.

01:19:28.260 --> 01:19:32.030
I was doing a sauna in Montana

01:19:32.030 --> 01:19:33.710
when we lived in Montana for five years

01:19:33.710 --> 01:19:38.330
as a accident, and a beautiful accident.

01:19:40.760 --> 01:19:42.800
Some nice photos from that though,

01:19:42.800 --> 01:19:43.989
came out of that.

01:19:43.989 --> 01:19:46.623
Yeah, we did it for the photos, did it for the gram.

01:19:48.708 --> 01:19:50.049
You did not do it for the gram.

01:19:50.049 --> 01:19:52.470
I don't think we even had Instagram.

01:19:52.470 --> 01:19:55.370
But anyway, I was in a sauna

01:19:55.370 --> 01:19:58.700
with an American Indian named Tom.

01:19:58.700 --> 01:20:03.520
And there's like a basketball court right next to sauna.

01:20:03.520 --> 01:20:05.320
It's the YMCA in Missoula.

01:20:05.320 --> 01:20:08.730
And the swimming and all this other stuff.

01:20:08.730 --> 01:20:11.787
And he goes, "You know, I don't understand you Americans,

01:20:11.787 --> 01:20:16.787
"you have this, this idea of in order for you to win,

01:20:17.967 --> 01:20:19.610
"you have to lose."

01:20:19.610 --> 01:20:22.307
He's like, "But where I come from, if you win, he wins.

01:20:22.307 --> 01:20:24.577
"And if he loses you lose."

01:20:25.430 --> 01:20:27.720
And of course my first knee jerk reaction

01:20:27.720 --> 01:20:31.900
to that was like, that's a silly way to think about life,

01:20:31.900 --> 01:20:33.680
because I'm winning.

01:20:33.680 --> 01:20:35.491
And it's like, well, no,

01:20:35.491 --> 01:20:37.520
You just haven't won enough, that's your problem.

01:20:37.520 --> 01:20:39.160
Yeah, but think about that.

01:20:39.160 --> 01:20:42.610
We talk about winning.

01:20:42.610 --> 01:20:46.320
Obviously Charlie Sheen is the parotic

01:20:46.320 --> 01:20:47.740
exaggeration of that.

01:20:47.740 --> 01:20:51.600
But that's not far off from where our culture is.

01:20:51.600 --> 01:20:54.230
We talk about winning as though it's a good thing.

01:20:54.230 --> 01:20:56.780
But by default, if someone wins, someone else has to lose.

01:20:56.780 --> 01:20:59.250
Now, maybe it's a semantic thing

01:20:59.250 --> 01:21:01.590
and we can change our language around it.

01:21:01.590 --> 01:21:03.040
But I think language is really important.

01:21:03.040 --> 01:21:04.910
I think it's a real big problem too

01:21:04.910 --> 01:21:06.283
but I think our language,

01:21:08.510 --> 01:21:10.522
it canvases our days.

01:21:10.522 --> 01:21:13.420
Language is responsible for more misery

01:21:13.420 --> 01:21:15.220
than anything else in human history.

01:21:16.240 --> 01:21:18.123
Well, that idea, I mean,

01:21:19.353 --> 01:21:21.560
the most successful people I know,

01:21:21.560 --> 01:21:22.490
maybe not all of them

01:21:22.490 --> 01:21:25.020
but I know a lot of successful people

01:21:25.020 --> 01:21:29.580
who do approach the world with that idea of largess

01:21:29.580 --> 01:21:31.640
that it's not a zero sum game.

01:21:31.640 --> 01:21:34.363
And if I win, you win too.

01:21:34.363 --> 01:21:39.320
And not only are you able to basically

01:21:39.320 --> 01:21:42.330
succeed in whatever it is that you're trying to do.

01:21:42.330 --> 01:21:45.400
Those people tend to be much happier people

01:21:45.400 --> 01:21:49.400
and grounded and more conscious in general.

01:21:49.400 --> 01:21:51.530
You had the Tom's guy on recently, Blake?

01:21:51.530 --> 01:21:52.550
Yeah, Blake.

01:21:52.550 --> 01:21:54.270
He strikes me as that.

01:21:54.270 --> 01:21:56.010
He's definitely someone like that.

01:21:56.010 --> 01:21:58.880
I also had Karamo from Queer Eye on

01:21:58.880 --> 01:22:02.656
and he was talking about the audition process for Queer Eye.

01:22:02.656 --> 01:22:06.730
And they spent like years trying to find

01:22:06.730 --> 01:22:10.530
the four dudes that do the show.

01:22:10.530 --> 01:22:13.830
And most of the guys were super competitive

01:22:13.830 --> 01:22:14.900
and they'd go in and audition.

01:22:14.900 --> 01:22:17.827
And then they come out and the other guys would be like,

01:22:17.827 --> 01:22:18.880
"What'd they ask you?"

01:22:18.880 --> 01:22:20.125
And they wouldn't say,

01:22:20.125 --> 01:22:21.870
'cause they're like, I'm competing against you.

01:22:21.870 --> 01:22:24.463
I'm not gonna tell you any secrets.

01:22:25.360 --> 01:22:26.770
But there were a couple of them

01:22:26.770 --> 01:22:27.820
that would come out and be like,

01:22:27.820 --> 01:22:30.510
here's what happened, here's what you need to look out for.

01:22:30.510 --> 01:22:32.560
And those were the guys that ultimately

01:22:32.560 --> 01:22:34.040
all ended up getting chosen

01:22:34.040 --> 01:22:35.373
Wow.

01:22:35.373 --> 01:22:36.467
For the show
That's incredible

01:22:36.467 --> 01:22:38.177
which obviously changed their lives

01:22:38.177 --> 01:22:40.876
and the lives of a lot of people that watch that show,

01:22:40.876 --> 01:22:41.875
Interesting.

01:22:41.875 --> 01:22:42.879
which is cool.

01:22:42.879 --> 01:22:44.054
That is cool

01:22:44.054 --> 01:22:45.137
I love that idea.

01:22:45.137 --> 01:22:46.356
It makes me think of Google,

01:22:46.356 --> 01:22:47.970
when they hire people they bring them on a bus

01:22:47.970 --> 01:22:50.210
and then whether or not they thank the bus driver

01:22:50.210 --> 01:22:53.620
is like a huge, it's like a litmus test.

01:22:53.620 --> 01:22:54.959
Oh, I see.

01:22:54.959 --> 01:22:55.980
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, that's interesting.

01:22:55.980 --> 01:22:58.370
Like, how do you behave when no one's watching?

01:22:58.370 --> 01:23:01.850
Because in this context there were casting people

01:23:01.850 --> 01:23:03.710
who are paying attention to this behavior.

01:23:03.710 --> 01:23:06.850
And it's like, we want people that approach the world

01:23:06.850 --> 01:23:10.640
with that kind of arms outstretched perspective

01:23:10.640 --> 01:23:12.760
of gratitude and giving

01:23:12.760 --> 01:23:13.593
Amen.

01:23:13.593 --> 01:23:15.210
And to reward that.

01:23:15.210 --> 01:23:17.360
We were talking about Dave Ramsey earlier

01:23:18.610 --> 01:23:20.900
his team, they interview the spouse

01:23:20.900 --> 01:23:21.930
in one of the interviews,

01:23:21.930 --> 01:23:23.770
it's a rather rigorous process

01:23:23.770 --> 01:23:26.833
but your spouse gets interviewed as well, separately.

01:23:29.760 --> 01:23:34.020
And it's fascinating to me because I think

01:23:34.020 --> 01:23:35.863
maybe they're like trying to figure out,

01:23:38.150 --> 01:23:39.430
how do you handle conflict?

01:23:39.430 --> 01:23:40.923
How do you make decisions?

01:23:42.350 --> 01:23:44.530
How do you make some of your biggest life choices?

01:23:44.530 --> 01:23:46.080
Are they congruent with the person

01:23:46.080 --> 01:23:48.603
that we want to to hire?

01:23:50.150 --> 01:23:52.280
Now I can imagine that might be a train wreck

01:23:52.280 --> 01:23:57.070
for some people, but I know if you interviewed Becks,

01:23:57.070 --> 01:24:00.254
I would totally get the job no matter what the job was.

01:24:00.254 --> 01:24:02.480
(all laughing)

01:24:02.480 --> 01:24:05.150
How do you know what she's gonna say?

01:24:05.150 --> 01:24:06.333
You think you know.

01:24:08.720 --> 01:24:10.860
Let's talk a little bit more about the movie.

01:24:10.860 --> 01:24:13.860
I mean, I came into this thinking

01:24:13.860 --> 01:24:15.950
these guys have been working on this thing for years,

01:24:15.950 --> 01:24:17.460
because I know how long ago it was

01:24:17.460 --> 01:24:19.680
when I was there at that event.

01:24:19.680 --> 01:24:23.350
But you are featuring these individuals,

01:24:23.350 --> 01:24:24.720
the kind of everyday people,

01:24:24.720 --> 01:24:27.110
minimalist people that are highlighted

01:24:27.110 --> 01:24:29.620
in the movie and they're on Zoom calls.

01:24:29.620 --> 01:24:30.650
So I was like, well, clearly

01:24:30.650 --> 01:24:33.710
you're executing this project during the pandemic.

01:24:33.710 --> 01:24:35.380
The pandemic was the best and worst thing

01:24:35.380 --> 01:24:36.968
that happened in this film.

01:24:36.968 --> 01:24:41.240
So we had this entire day set up in Los Angeles.

01:24:41.240 --> 01:24:43.330
We had a whole crew booked.

01:24:43.330 --> 01:24:46.100
We had location and we were bringing

01:24:46.100 --> 01:24:49.210
I don't know what it was, 22 or 32 people

01:24:49.210 --> 01:24:50.930
in to be interviewed that day

01:24:50.930 --> 01:24:53.821
the majority of them were these everyday minimalists.

01:24:53.821 --> 01:24:58.720
In fact, I think it was a two day shoot early or mid March.

01:24:58.720 --> 01:25:00.890
Of course, you know what happened.

01:25:00.890 --> 01:25:02.087
Right at the beginning,

01:25:02.087 --> 01:25:04.290
the starting line of the whole thing.

01:25:04.290 --> 01:25:08.563
But the original thing that we wanted to do was,

01:25:11.330 --> 01:25:12.670
have you seen YouTube reaction videos

01:25:12.670 --> 01:25:15.010
when people sort of react to a music video

01:25:15.010 --> 01:25:17.870
or they react to a product or service or something?

01:25:17.870 --> 01:25:19.850
I might've seen one or two of those.

01:25:19.850 --> 01:25:22.500
So we kind of wanted to do that,

01:25:22.500 --> 01:25:24.860
but with minimalism in a way.

01:25:24.860 --> 01:25:26.700
And so it was Chris's idea.

01:25:26.700 --> 01:25:30.010
Chris is our director of photography on the film.

01:25:30.010 --> 01:25:33.880
And so that actually worked out better

01:25:33.880 --> 01:25:36.410
by bringing us sort of into their homes in a way.

01:25:36.410 --> 01:25:38.460
In fact, we had them do some B roll

01:25:38.460 --> 01:25:39.420
of some of their houses,

01:25:39.420 --> 01:25:40.850
that shows up in the film as well.

01:25:40.850 --> 01:25:43.360
So you get to see the sort of decluttering process

01:25:43.360 --> 01:25:44.564
they went through.

01:25:44.564 --> 01:25:45.460
Some of them even had footage that they took

01:25:45.460 --> 01:25:49.300
when they simplified their life a year or two ago.

01:25:49.300 --> 01:25:51.860
And we got to include all of that in the film,

01:25:51.860 --> 01:25:53.830
even though we filmed the rest of it in the studio

01:25:53.830 --> 01:25:58.040
Well, it gives it in addition to this verite touch,

01:25:58.040 --> 01:26:01.080
it's heartwarming because it's so authentic.

01:26:01.080 --> 01:26:03.350
It's like, they're actually shooting it themselves

01:26:03.350 --> 01:26:06.860
and sharing it as opposed to a sterilized film crew

01:26:06.860 --> 01:26:10.600
coming in and trying to make everything look just so.

01:26:10.600 --> 01:26:11.830
We sent them cameras and stuff.

01:26:11.830 --> 01:26:15.730
So it looked congruent with,

01:26:15.730 --> 01:26:18.200
each one didn't feel like it was out of place

01:26:18.200 --> 01:26:20.810
but yeah, it was them doing the thing on their own.

01:26:20.810 --> 01:26:23.680
Yeah, and we got most of the cameras back.

01:26:23.680 --> 01:26:25.015
I'm just kidding.

01:26:25.015 --> 01:26:25.848
We got them all back.

01:26:27.040 --> 01:26:27.933
More stuff.

01:26:29.680 --> 01:26:32.300
Talk to me about some of the other experts

01:26:32.300 --> 01:26:33.390
that you have featured in here.

01:26:33.390 --> 01:26:36.260
'Cause it's an interesting grouping of people.

01:26:36.260 --> 01:26:37.390
In addition to Dave Ramsey,

01:26:37.390 --> 01:26:40.180
you've got TK Coleman who I know

01:26:40.180 --> 01:26:42.040
he was on your podcast not that long ago,

01:26:42.040 --> 01:26:44.230
Annie Leonard from Greenpeace,

01:26:44.230 --> 01:26:47.950
Denaye Barahona from Simple Families.

01:26:47.950 --> 01:26:51.330
So she's kind of squarely in the minimalist space,

01:26:51.330 --> 01:26:52.163
it feels like.

01:26:52.163 --> 01:26:53.520
But it was intriguing,

01:26:53.520 --> 01:26:55.480
like, oh, this person from Greenpeace,

01:26:55.480 --> 01:26:57.680
she's about sustainability,

01:26:57.680 --> 01:27:01.323
but I was interested in how you made these choices.

01:27:03.191 --> 01:27:05.230
She did the story of stuff, if you recall.

01:27:05.230 --> 01:27:07.540
She's the lady who did that video.

01:27:07.540 --> 01:27:09.890
I mean, it's been seen probably 100 million times

01:27:09.890 --> 01:27:11.301
at this point, The Story of Stuff.

01:27:11.301 --> 01:27:12.134
That's weird, 'cause I saw her and I was like,

01:27:12.134 --> 01:27:13.563
she looks really familiar.

01:27:14.570 --> 01:27:17.160
There's an actress that looks kind of like her as well

01:27:17.160 --> 01:27:18.510
but I couldn't put my finger on it.

01:27:18.510 --> 01:27:22.650
Yeah, so she's an executive director of Greenpeace USA,

01:27:22.650 --> 01:27:27.650
And yeah, so we only wanted five experts in the film

01:27:27.790 --> 01:27:30.240
and we wanted them from these different perspectives.

01:27:30.240 --> 01:27:32.210
So one was the environmental side of things.

01:27:32.210 --> 01:27:35.340
There's obviously overlap with all of them.

01:27:35.340 --> 01:27:36.880
We wanted the economic side of things.

01:27:36.880 --> 01:27:41.453
So TK Coleman, he is an education director at FEE.

01:27:42.540 --> 01:27:44.897
We had Dave Ramsey as sort of the money side of things,

01:27:44.897 --> 01:27:48.340
the debt especially, how it's tied to consumerism.

01:27:48.340 --> 01:27:51.940
Denaye Barahona, she runs an organization

01:27:51.940 --> 01:27:53.220
called Simple Families,

01:27:53.220 --> 01:27:55.770
and really focused on parenting and kids.

01:27:55.770 --> 01:27:58.520
And so we wanted to bring the family side in there as well.

01:27:58.520 --> 01:28:00.090
And we had Erwin McManus

01:28:00.090 --> 01:28:03.120
who is a pastor here in Los Angeles,

01:28:03.120 --> 01:28:05.790
who probably was, it's hard to pick a favorite,

01:28:05.790 --> 01:28:08.153
but he had some of my favorite lines in the entire film.

01:28:08.153 --> 01:28:09.363
He's an interesting dude.

01:28:09.363 --> 01:28:11.750
' Cause wasn't he like a fashion designer

01:28:11.750 --> 01:28:12.583
or something like that?
Yes.

01:28:12.583 --> 01:28:15.200
He has this whole other, he's like a designer.

01:28:15.200 --> 01:28:16.500
He does all this other stuff.

01:28:16.500 --> 01:28:18.170
Yeah, and he still does.

01:28:18.170 --> 01:28:21.913
He just launched a new, like super minimalist clothing line.

01:28:23.440 --> 01:28:28.440
And he talks about intentional living

01:28:28.570 --> 01:28:32.510
in ways that, they're just really profound.

01:28:32.510 --> 01:28:34.830
And I was just really grateful to have that opportunity

01:28:34.830 --> 01:28:36.276
to sit with him.

01:28:36.276 --> 01:28:37.440
He's been on our podcast a couple of times to.

01:28:37.440 --> 01:28:38.880
TK has been on our podcast.

01:28:38.880 --> 01:28:41.550
I think you've been on there the second most.

01:28:41.550 --> 01:28:44.383
TK has been on there maybe like eight times.

01:28:45.990 --> 01:28:48.230
In fact, if I can introduce you to anyone,

01:28:48.230 --> 01:28:49.569
TK, he'll blow your socks off,

01:28:49.569 --> 01:28:50.402
Oh cool.

01:28:50.402 --> 01:28:51.690
the best person you could speak to

01:28:51.690 --> 01:28:52.900
about just about anything,

01:28:52.900 --> 01:28:54.460
but especially like economics.

01:28:54.460 --> 01:28:56.330
But I'm unclear on his, like, so he's in education,

01:28:56.330 --> 01:28:58.220
but I'm unclear about the nexus

01:28:58.220 --> 01:29:00.460
between that and minimalism.

01:29:00.460 --> 01:29:04.330
So he was sort of our economy expert in a way.

01:29:04.330 --> 01:29:07.970
And I wanted to juxtapose,

01:29:07.970 --> 01:29:09.600
you could call him a capitalist,

01:29:09.600 --> 01:29:11.340
though that word doesn't really mean

01:29:11.340 --> 01:29:13.420
what you would,

01:29:13.420 --> 01:29:18.420
it's taken on a pejorative frame in the last five, 10 years,

01:29:19.090 --> 01:29:21.410
but I wanted to juxtapose Annie

01:29:21.410 --> 01:29:25.270
who has probably radically different beliefs

01:29:25.270 --> 01:29:28.060
from TK and show that they overlap.

01:29:28.060 --> 01:29:30.660
And it literally overlapped in the film together.

01:29:30.660 --> 01:29:32.330
And even though they disagreed about some things,

01:29:32.330 --> 01:29:34.360
where she talks about growth and infinite growth

01:29:34.360 --> 01:29:37.186
and then he immediately sort of rebuts that

01:29:37.186 --> 01:29:40.090
by talking about, I don't think the problem is growth.

01:29:40.090 --> 01:29:43.150
And he goes into what he thinks the problem is.

01:29:43.150 --> 01:29:46.740
And so we wanted some opinions that,

01:29:46.740 --> 01:29:49.470
we didn't want it to be like the Yes Man show.

01:29:49.470 --> 01:29:52.480
I wanted to learn something from these people.

01:29:52.480 --> 01:29:56.770
And Erwin, he really helps solidify the theme of the film.

01:29:56.770 --> 01:29:59.520
It's a film, as Ryan said earlier, about starting over.

01:30:00.489 --> 01:30:01.487
He has a line in there

01:30:01.487 --> 01:30:02.370
about the shaking the Etch-a-Sketch thing

01:30:02.370 --> 01:30:04.146
that Ryan talked about,

01:30:04.146 --> 01:30:09.146
but we should all, not should, there's no should.

01:30:09.470 --> 01:30:11.050
We all have the opportunity.

01:30:11.050 --> 01:30:13.562
I like how you caught yourself there.

01:30:13.562 --> 01:30:15.870
Here's the most problem I have is like

01:30:15.870 --> 01:30:18.560
I keep setting these things down, like the shoulds.

01:30:18.560 --> 01:30:20.090
And then I have the pattern.

01:30:20.090 --> 01:30:22.430
I pick them back up repeatedly.

01:30:22.430 --> 01:30:23.380
We all have the opportunity though,

01:30:23.380 --> 01:30:25.950
to restart our lives, to start over.

01:30:25.950 --> 01:30:28.977
And this film was about starting over with less.

01:30:28.977 --> 01:30:30.300
And that can be less stuff,

01:30:30.300 --> 01:30:33.170
but it can also be less distractions,

01:30:33.170 --> 01:30:35.550
fewer commitments, et cetera.

01:30:35.550 --> 01:30:36.383
I love that.

01:30:36.383 --> 01:30:37.216
Yeah.

01:30:37.216 --> 01:30:40.310
What is the main idea that you want

01:30:40.310 --> 01:30:42.393
people to take away from the film?

01:30:44.420 --> 01:30:45.510
I mean, I think Josh said it,

01:30:45.510 --> 01:30:46.440
it's about starting over.

01:30:46.440 --> 01:30:48.610
I mean, is this film for every single person?

01:30:48.610 --> 01:30:51.031
I think every single person will get something out of it

01:30:51.031 --> 01:30:53.880
but I think who it's going to help the most

01:30:53.880 --> 01:30:55.770
is someone who's in a situation right now

01:30:55.770 --> 01:30:59.560
and they need some emotional leverage to start over.

01:30:59.560 --> 01:31:02.394
I think this film will help them do that.

01:31:02.394 --> 01:31:03.744
Just the nudge they need.

01:31:07.289 --> 01:31:09.120
I don't think the film was for everyone,

01:31:09.120 --> 01:31:11.450
as Ryan just said, but it's for anyone

01:31:11.450 --> 01:31:15.040
who's sort of dissatisfied by the status quo

01:31:15.040 --> 01:31:16.750
whatever status quo they've created for themselves.

01:31:16.750 --> 01:31:18.383
We're overburdened.

01:31:18.383 --> 01:31:21.010
And a lot of that has to do with stuff.

01:31:21.010 --> 01:31:23.960
But I think it also has to do with toxic relationships.

01:31:23.960 --> 01:31:25.620
I think it has to do with debt.

01:31:25.620 --> 01:31:27.550
There are all these burdens, we've taken them on.

01:31:27.550 --> 01:31:29.840
We've picked up all this baggage,

01:31:29.840 --> 01:31:31.690
but we can also set it down.

01:31:31.690 --> 01:31:33.040
It might take some time to set it down,

01:31:33.040 --> 01:31:34.890
especially with debt.

01:31:34.890 --> 01:31:36.440
In the first film, in Minimalism,

01:31:36.440 --> 01:31:37.660
I talked about,

01:31:37.660 --> 01:31:40.450
I had almost half a million dollars worth of debt.

01:31:40.450 --> 01:31:43.540
I was making $200 grand a year in Dayton, Ohio

01:31:43.540 --> 01:31:46.140
but I was spending like $220 grand a year,

01:31:46.140 --> 01:31:47.870
whatever they would let me spend.

01:31:47.870 --> 01:31:51.010
And so I had massive amounts of debt as a result.

01:31:51.010 --> 01:31:55.420
And I had to sort of set that burden down.

01:31:55.420 --> 01:31:57.513
It took some time, but you know,

01:31:59.189 --> 01:32:01.700
the best way do that is to stop spending,

01:32:01.700 --> 01:32:04.400
to stop whatever excess is going on.

01:32:04.400 --> 01:32:08.090
We can't add our way to contentment,

01:32:08.090 --> 01:32:10.150
to joy, to peace.

01:32:10.150 --> 01:32:12.713
It's always about subtraction.

01:32:13.790 --> 01:32:18.790
The timing is very interesting because we're all at home.

01:32:19.680 --> 01:32:23.400
And I think with that comes a natural inclination

01:32:23.400 --> 01:32:25.710
to do a little bit of inventory

01:32:25.710 --> 01:32:26.990
on how we're living our lives

01:32:26.990 --> 01:32:28.840
and what is our relationship to our job,

01:32:28.840 --> 01:32:29.700
to our profession,

01:32:29.700 --> 01:32:31.720
when you're not going into an office every day

01:32:31.720 --> 01:32:33.880
or into a workplace in the manner

01:32:33.880 --> 01:32:35.600
in which you're accustomed to,

01:32:35.600 --> 01:32:38.660
that interruption of the flow or the routine

01:32:38.660 --> 01:32:40.820
I think is triggering a lot of people

01:32:40.820 --> 01:32:44.130
to be more reflective about how they're spending their time

01:32:44.130 --> 01:32:46.530
and their resources, et cetera.

01:32:46.530 --> 01:32:49.180
So to the point about the nudge

01:32:49.180 --> 01:32:54.180
or the kind of gentle push towards these ideas,

01:32:55.700 --> 01:32:57.950
I feel like people are primed for this now.

01:32:57.950 --> 01:33:02.023
And it is a moment of radical change.

01:33:04.850 --> 01:33:06.420
A lot of people are thinking about this

01:33:06.420 --> 01:33:09.240
in terms of when are we gonna get back to the way it was.

01:33:09.240 --> 01:33:11.340
It's not going back to the way it was.

01:33:11.340 --> 01:33:13.190
We are forever altered.

01:33:13.190 --> 01:33:15.930
Some things will normalize to a certain extent

01:33:15.930 --> 01:33:17.850
but the idea that everyone's gonna migrate

01:33:17.850 --> 01:33:20.610
back into office buildings, I think is lunacy.

01:33:20.610 --> 01:33:21.590
That's not gonna happen.

01:33:21.590 --> 01:33:25.650
We've now figured out how to pursue livings

01:33:25.650 --> 01:33:28.150
from Zoom and these tools that we have.

01:33:28.150 --> 01:33:30.060
So what does that look like down the line?

01:33:30.060 --> 01:33:33.500
And how can we reflect on this

01:33:33.500 --> 01:33:38.500
to reform our relationship with the outside world

01:33:39.180 --> 01:33:40.350
so that it can be healthier

01:33:40.350 --> 01:33:42.380
and it can be more fulfilling and more purposeful?

01:33:42.380 --> 01:33:45.877
And the movie really speaks to that in a profound way.

01:33:45.877 --> 01:33:48.500
And I think it's gonna help a lot of people.

01:33:48.500 --> 01:33:50.115
Thank you.

01:33:50.115 --> 01:33:51.112
Yeah, thanks.

01:33:51.112 --> 01:33:52.810
Normal wasn't working for a lot of us

01:33:52.810 --> 01:33:54.970
and this gave us the opportunity to realize that.

01:33:54.970 --> 01:33:56.860
It was a forced pause for many of us

01:33:56.860 --> 01:33:58.540
even for me and Ryan.

01:33:58.540 --> 01:34:00.330
We had a whole tour planned this year

01:34:00.330 --> 01:34:03.663
and the film was gonna come out earlier, et cetera.

01:34:04.980 --> 01:34:07.050
And we had a bunch of speaking things

01:34:07.050 --> 01:34:08.710
we were going to do.

01:34:08.710 --> 01:34:10.390
There aren't corporations that are meeting,

01:34:10.390 --> 01:34:13.123
so we couldn't go speak at these places.

01:34:14.900 --> 01:34:17.620
And so it was a pause for us as well

01:34:17.620 --> 01:34:20.910
but it's not to take away either

01:34:23.708 --> 01:34:24.880
what some people are faced with.

01:34:24.880 --> 01:34:28.440
My brother, he is back in Ohio, he works in a factory.

01:34:28.440 --> 01:34:31.180
He worked in a factory building cabinets.

01:34:31.180 --> 01:34:35.590
It was a well-paying job and he was talented and skilled.

01:34:35.590 --> 01:34:37.460
And he was doing that.

01:34:37.460 --> 01:34:38.790
That whole thing shut down.

01:34:38.790 --> 01:34:39.830
It's not coming back.

01:34:39.830 --> 01:34:42.320
He went and got part-time job at Amazon

01:34:42.320 --> 01:34:45.061
so he could pay, feed his daughters.

01:34:45.061 --> 01:34:48.080
And now he's working third shift at a meat packing plant

01:34:48.080 --> 01:34:50.900
because that's like his only reality right now.

01:34:50.900 --> 01:34:54.060
And talk about one of the most difficult jobs you could have

01:34:56.080 --> 01:34:59.210
and doing it third shift, no less.

01:34:59.210 --> 01:35:01.970
But so a lot of people are affected by this.

01:35:01.970 --> 01:35:06.970
And so, while I don't want to go back to where we were,

01:35:07.050 --> 01:35:09.983
I also don't want to see more suffering.

01:35:11.060 --> 01:35:14.730
And so I think what's happening right now

01:35:14.730 --> 01:35:17.020
is Ryan and I for the last decade

01:35:17.020 --> 01:35:20.100
have been asking this question, what is essential?

01:35:20.100 --> 01:35:22.170
And now a lot more people are all of a sudden asking it.

01:35:22.170 --> 01:35:25.723
In fact, the term's even out there, essential worker, right.

01:35:27.480 --> 01:35:28.880
But what is essential in my home?

01:35:28.880 --> 01:35:29.990
What is essential in my life?

01:35:29.990 --> 01:35:32.420
What is essential on my calendar?

01:35:32.420 --> 01:35:33.706
What is essential?

01:35:33.706 --> 01:35:36.770
And that's what I hope to illuminate with this film,

01:35:36.770 --> 01:35:38.010
how people ask that question.

01:35:38.010 --> 01:35:39.433
What is essential?

01:35:41.224 --> 01:35:44.020
I think that's a good place to put a pin in it for now

01:35:44.020 --> 01:35:45.610
until you guys come back next time.

01:35:45.610 --> 01:35:47.064
Rich, I Love your brother.

01:35:47.064 --> 01:35:48.608
Thank you so much.
You're awesome.

01:35:48.608 --> 01:35:49.441
You guys too.

01:35:49.441 --> 01:35:51.690
I have so much love for you guys as people

01:35:51.690 --> 01:35:53.790
and respect for this mission

01:35:53.790 --> 01:35:55.860
that you've shouldered for the world.

01:35:55.860 --> 01:35:58.040
You guys are great servants to humanity

01:35:58.040 --> 01:35:59.250
and it's a privilege to talk to you.

01:35:59.250 --> 01:36:00.130
So thank you.

01:36:00.130 --> 01:36:01.784
Right back at you, brother.

01:36:01.784 --> 01:36:03.260
Wind in your sails, my friends.

01:36:03.260 --> 01:36:04.718
Thank you, brother.

01:36:04.718 --> 01:36:05.551
That means a lot.
Thank you so much.

